@node General Principles
@section General Principles

@enumerate
@item
@anchor{Full Responsibility and Accountability}Full Responsibility and Accountability


@itemize
@item
You @strong{MUST} take total ownership of every word@comma{} figure@comma{} and line of
code; verify all AI-assisted output against primary sources.
@item
You @strong{MUST NOT} assume that the AI knows best (e.g.@comma{} using ``The AI said
so'' as a justification)@comma{} that details do not matter@comma{} or that examiners
will not notice.
@item
You @strong{SHOULD} make sure you understand the output of your AI tool and
then write/use your own interpretation of it.
@item
@strong{Motivation}: You are effectively ``signing'' your assignments as your
intellectual output. You must be able to explain@comma{} justify@comma{} and trace
the origin of any part of it. In group work@comma{} accountability is
collective; all authors are responsible for the entire content.
@item
@strong{Example}: A student experimented with ChatGPT for generating some
program code for a task@comma{} but then re-typed it by themselves on a level
where they understand what the code does.
@end itemize

@item
@anchor{Do not Plagiarise}Do not Plagiarise


@itemize
@item
@strong{Comment}: This is a wider question than just the use of GenAI@comma{} but it
applies equally to material produced by GenAI tools.
@item
You @strong{MUST} properly cite and credit any material used in your
assignments. This includes the @emph{work} of others@comma{} and this includes
output from GenAI tools.
@item
You @strong{MUST NOT} copy-paste or otherwise present the work of others as
if it was your own.
@item
You @strong{SHOULD} make it a habit to always summarise text that you want to
use and to immediately add a reference to where you got it from.
@item
You @strong{MAY} include the prompts you used to generate code or text from a
GenAI tool@comma{} in order to discuss it with your teacher.
@item
@strong{Motivation 1}: The assignments are an opportunity for you to show
what @emph{you} have learned. By including sources and/or prompts we are
able to help you learn more.
@item
@strong{Motivation 2}: Plagiarism is a serious academic offence: You @emph{must
always} give credit to the original author(s).
@item
@strong{Example}: A student asks Claude to generate different suggestions for
the code for a particular task@comma{} but then writes their own version
which is a combination of several suggestions. They add the prompts
used in a source code comment and discuss these with the teacher.
@item
@strong{Example}: A student finds several ready-made solutions to a
programming task online@comma{} and writes their own solution inspired by
what they have read. They include links to the used pages in a source
code comment.
@end itemize

@item
@anchor{Use AI Tools to Explain not to Do}Use AI Tools to Explain@comma{} not to Do.


@itemize
@item
You @strong{MAY} use AI to explain different commands and overall workflow.
@item
You @strong{MAY} see this as an opportunity to pratice your ability to find
and read documentation and instruction manuals instead of relying on
AI tools.
@item
You @strong{SHOULD} make sure that you understand the tools that you use.
@item
You @strong{SHOULD} verify what the AI tells you through independent tests.
@item
You @strong{SHOULD NOT} rely on that the GenAI understand the tools for you.
@item
You @strong{MUST NOT} blame the AI for any mistakes it does. Those mistakes
are yours to catch.
@item
@strong{Motivation}: You must at least be able to identify when GenAI tools
suggests something wrong. Your value as an engineer is further
increased if you are also able to work independently with minimal tool
or AI support.
@item
@strong{Example}: An engineer asked an AI tool to generate code to implement
a feature and realised that the generated code would break the running
system and cost millions of dollars. If they had not independently
reviewed the generated code before committing they would not have
caught this.
@item
@strong{Comment}: In industry@comma{} the line between explaining and doing is
blurred@comma{} but SHOULD only be crossed by engineers @emph{who already know
what they are doing}.
@end itemize
@end enumerate

