@node A Note on Reading Research Articles
@chapter A Note on Reading Research Articles

This course may suggest research articles for you to read. This may seem a daunting task for you@comma{} but there are some general guidelines that may help you:

@itemize
@item
Start by reading the abstract and the conclusions. These will tell you what problem or area the article is addressing@comma{} and what the article contributed to solving the problem.
@item
The articles in this course have -- to a large extent -- been chosen because they summarise the topics in various ways. To find these summaries@comma{} look for @emph{lists@comma{} figures@comma{} and tables} in the article. Read these. Read the text around them@comma{} or where the figures and tables were referenced@comma{} in order to get an explanation of how to interpret them.
@end itemize


Generally@comma{} interpret each section of an article as follows:

@itemize
@item
The @emph{introduction} section in an article puts the problem into context@comma{} and might give you clues as to how other researchers have solved it before. You can use this section to get a generic overview.
@item
The @emph{related work} section should discuss in further detail what other researchers have done that is similar to@comma{} or relates to@comma{} the article you are reading. This may give you an idea of how others have addressed the problem@comma{} and may give you pointers to other articles that you would wish to read.
@item
The @emph{methodology} section explains how the study was conducted. Once an article is published (which they naturally are in this course)@comma{} this is mostly interesting if you distrust some of the results and want to see if anything has been missed when constructing the study.
@item
The @emph{execution and results} sections describe how the study was executed (in particular discrepancies from the planned methodology)@comma{} and what the raw results were. Most of the time@comma{} you can safely skip these sections. Sometimes however@comma{} the @emph{results} section is combined with the @emph{analysis} section.
@item
The @emph{analysis} section ``bakes'' the results and tries to answer the research questions (address the identified problem). This is probably the most interesting section for you to read.
@item
The @emph{discussion} section should raise the view and try to see what the results actually mean in a bigger context. What can you do with the results? ``So what?''. This is a tricky section to read@comma{} since the authors want you to believe that their results are the best thing invented since hot porridge@comma{} but at the same time they have to identify threats to the validity of the study. Validity threats are things that could have influenced the results instead of the sought after effect. This may be things that happened at the same time@comma{} inadequacies in the researchers skills and abilities@comma{} or inadequacies in the research design. Please remember@comma{} when (or if) reading this section@comma{} that most of the identified validity threats are minor obstacles (or the article would not have been published) that may impact the scientific view of the results more than the practical significance or usefulness of them.
@item
The @emph{references} may give you ideas for other articles that you would like to read.
@end itemize

The bottom line is that when being told to read a lot of research articles@comma{} the trick is to learn how to not read them while still getting the gist of them. Hopefully@comma{} the ``map'' described above may provide some help in identifying the parts of an article that are important for you.

@node Introduction to Orchestration and Provisioning
@chapter Introduction to Orchestration and Provisioning

@anchor{introduction}

A vital part of making the best use of cloud resources is to have a controllable way to set up your cloud environment. Ironically@comma{} a large part of this consists of @emph{not} using cloud resources but rather configure a transparent way to set up a local development environment@comma{} a potentially local test environment@comma{} and the production environment. The goal is to be able to do this with the same command(s) and to make sure that all environments are similarly configured.

You may be used to develop applications in your local development environment. From within this environment you will run and debug your application. You may even have set up automated tests to run as well. There are some challenges with this@comma{} however:

@itemize
@item
Your development computer needs to remain very stable. If you install some new software or run some update@comma{} you can no longer be sure that the application will actually continue to run. Even if it still works on your machine@comma{} there is no guarantee that it will also work on the customers' machine.
@end itemize

@cindex Configuration Management
@itemize
@item
When you start working on a new feature branch@comma{} your machine is indeed tainted by everything you have developed and tested before. Partially@comma{} this is what you have your configuration management system for: You would go back to the main branch and create your new feature branch from there. Technically@comma{} this @emph{should} remove everything else you have been doing so that you start from the same page every time. In practice@comma{} the settings in your development environment is not affected by what you do with the configuration management tool@comma{} your stack of installed software is not affected@comma{} and if you use a database it will merrily continue with whatever test data you had in there just before.
@end itemize

@cindex Deployment
@itemize
@item
When you are ready to deploy your application (of course you have already tested it?)@comma{} you first need to set this server up and make sure that it is installed and setup in exactly the same way as your development machine. And then you deploy. And say a prayer. And hope that no users try to use your application while you are figuring out what went wrong.
@end itemize

@cindex Redeployment
@itemize
@item
As time goes@comma{} you ever so often log in to the server with your running application to install updates@comma{} fix minor errors@comma{} and upgrade your application. Until things have gone horribly wrong and you need to start from scratch again@comma{} and you realise that you have not maintained an updated lab notebook with all the things you have ever done on the server. Things that you have already fixed once re-appear and you have to fix them again. And again.
@end itemize

@cindex Deployment Environments
This brings us back to the overall goal. What you want is a controllable and repeatable way to set up your machines. Controllable@comma{} so that you can decide whether you want a development-version (where perhaps your application is running with debug flags turned on)@comma{} a test-version (that contains a reasonable subset of all the data in the live system and where tests for different environments are launched automatically)@comma{} or a running production version of your system (that you really have no business ever editing directly@comma{} and maybe never need to edit directly since it is deployed automatically as soon as all tests are run).

@cindex Repeatable Deployment
You want the setup to be @emph{repeatable} so that you will always start from the same page@comma{} no matter what. Basically@comma{} you want to banish even the idea that it is ok to log in to a running environment to change things. If you want to introduce changes@comma{} you change the repeatable scripts@comma{} test them@comma{} and then push them to the rest of the project for common use. Now@comma{} @emph{anyone} in the project can create the exact same environment as you have@comma{} and test your code under exactly the same conditions.

@cindex Configuration Managed Deployments
Today@comma{} you would not even dream of working in a larger development project without having some form of configuration management support. You create feature development branches@comma{} merge features into testing branches@comma{} which eventually are merged into a main branch. Your automated tests are treated in the same way; The test code may even be part of the same commits as the application code. By creating scripts for your deployments@comma{} you now have the ability to put these too under configuration management. No more guesswork about which version of framework ``frobnicator'' that was launched together with version 1.3.42 of your application -- it's in the setup scripts.

@strong{Summary}
@cindex Goal -- a Controllable and Repeatable Deployment
@cindex Ultimate Goal -- Deploy Application to a Cloud Server
The @emph{ultimate} goal remains to be able to deploy your application to a cloud server somewhere. But the @emph{path to get there} require you to spend time and effort to set up a repeatable foundation to develop locally. As always@comma{} by laying a proper foundation it is much easier to reach the end goal.

In this part of the course we are thus going to be looking at different ways of setting up local development in a controllable and repeatable way. We are going to look into different types of deployments (e.g. microservices or infrastructure-as-a-service)@comma{} and different types of deployment targets (local@comma{} test@comma{} production).

We are going to do this through a combination of theoretical knowledge and practical exercises. The practical exercises will allow you to try out different tools for creating different types of deployments.

@cindex Command-Line Interface
@strong{A word of warning} There are many tools to master@comma{} and each of them use their own language. We are going to be working in a console (Command Prompt@comma{} terminal@comma{} xterm@comma{} or whatever your operating system provides) using the command line interfaces of these tools. The reason for this is that we want to create a solution that can be combined into a bigger scripted solution@comma{} and this is simply not possible if you are expected to intervene and point-and-click your way to a solution.

