@node Microservices and Lightweight Containers
@chapter Microservices and Lightweight Containers

As applications grow@comma{} it becomes more and more difficult to understand all of them at once. The programming solution is to divide the application into components@comma{} where each component has well defined responsibilities and interfaces. Component-based software engineering and code modularisation (and -- as it is most often used -- object oriented software development) is an answer to the need for smaller@comma{} more manageable parts of a larger system. However@comma{} ultimately@comma{} the main reason for this type of modularisation is @emph{development} needs.

@cindex Software Architecture Conceptual View
@cindex Software Architecture Module View
@cindex Software Architecture Execution View
Going further down the rabbit hole of software architectures@comma{} we do see that there are other reasons for modularisation. One often discuss different viewpoints of a software architecture (which -- just like UML -- has its origins in P@. Kruchten ``The 4+ 1 view model of architecture.'' @emph{IEEE software} 12.6 (1995): 42-50. )@comma{} with a @emph{conceptual view} to understand what to build@comma{} a @emph{module view} to understand how to structure it while building@comma{} and an @emph{execution view} to understand the threads@comma{} processes@comma{} processors@comma{} memory@comma{} and shared memory required in order to run the system. These are not created in isolation; the module view in particular need to reflect both the conceptual view as well as the execution view in order to build the right execution targets so that the application can be properly deployed.

Thus: as an application grows in size and complexity@comma{} there is a need to structure it into modules for the sake of conceptual understanding and for the sake of scalability.

@cindex Client Server Software Architecture
Some types of execution modularisation is probably already familiar to you. You have probably heard of @emph{client-server architectures}@comma{} and you have probably built systems using an external database. If you are unlucky@comma{} you may have developed multi-threaded applications.

@cindex Microservice Architecture
@emph{Microservice architectures} is an attempt to simplify development. On an architecture level@comma{} it is an attempt to settle on a single type of interface between the different conceptual components (usually a @ref{Communicating Microservices and REST APIs, , REST API})@comma{} align the execution of an application with its conceptual view@comma{} and defer creation of the module view to each individual component/executable. Of course@comma{} this works less well in practice because one still wishes to re-use components between the different microservices but that's another story.

One more benefit (some would argue it is the main benefit) is that there is no longer any need for multi-threaded programming. Each microservice can (and perhaps even should be) as single-threaded as possible. If one needs to scale the application this is done as part of the deployment rather than with the help of logic inside the application. This creates a @emph{separation of concern} between application logic and deployment logic which is highly sought after.

Another benefit is that each microservice can be written in the way and language best suited for its particular responsibility. Some may use a particular framework that dictate how to structure the code@comma{} others may be written in some high performance language@comma{} and others again may consist of a simple stringing together of a couple of Unix commands.

@cindex Governance
A third benefit is that with the separation between application logic and deployment logic it is easy to attach monitors to each microservice to gauge e.g. whether it is running@comma{} healthy@comma{} or overloaded. Logs can be created and inspected within each microservice@comma{} and temporary or permanent storage may be attached to each microservice.

