@node Building Microservices
@section Building Microservices

What do we then need to do in order to build our application as a collection of collaborating microservices?

In the most basic level@comma{} we simply build our application as a collection of executables. We launch each executable as a separate process@comma{} and have some mechanism to communicate between the processes (e.g. sockets@comma{} remote procedure calls@comma{} pipes@comma{} file system@comma{} or through the database). Challenges are that we still have to start and connect all the different parts of our system when running@comma{} and we need to build the logic ourselves to scale up or down. If we want to scale one part of our application by starting more processes of the same type@comma{} we need to manage the logic for this. If we want to ``scale out'' to another machine@comma{} we need to implement and manage the logic for this. If one process writes to a particular file@comma{} all other processes are directly influenced by this@comma{} and need to have logic implemented to avoid conflicts.

@pindex chroot
Next level up@comma{} at least on UNIX systems@comma{} is through @samp{chroot}. Through this program we basically ``sandbox'' the file system for a particular process so that we can have separate parts of our disk allocated to different processes (called a ``chroot jail'' ). Within this new root environment we can install tools only needed for this particular process. We can also have separate sections of our disk for e.g. testing environments and development environments@comma{} and we can isolate all dependencies from one process to only that which is available within its particular chroot jail.

@samp{chroot} is old@comma{} it was created in 1979@comma{} and as computers became more powerful it started to be used as a lightweight virtualisation. There are@comma{} however@comma{} some considerable limitations. For the sake of this course@comma{} the most major limitation is that any logic surrounding the use of chroot -- scaling@comma{} inter process communication@comma{} etc. -- still has to be implemented by the developers of the application. Shirley there's an app to help with that!

@pindex LXC Linux Containers
@pindex Docker
@pindex Podman
@cindex Container
As it happens@comma{} there are applications for this. You may have heard about Docker @uref{https://www.docker.com/} or its open source cousin Podman @uref{https://podman.io/}. Both of these are examples of Linux Containers (LXC)@comma{} which are usually described as ``Lightweight virtual machines''. With tools like these you create a @emph{Container} with a specific set of programs installed@comma{} and then you can manage this container as a whole@comma{} rather than having to keep track of each individual part inside.

@cindex Responsibility-Driven Design
This container essentially consist of two things: (a) The software needed to run your microservice@comma{} and (b) your microservice@comma{} and the idea is to keep both of these as small as possible. Do not expect a full user experience from the software stack in a container@comma{} be prepared to fight with @code{sh} rather than something slightly easier to use like @code{bash}. As for the microservice that you deploy in the container@comma{} try to maintain a strict responsibility-driven design: If you want to give a container two responsibilities then consider splitting it into two containers.

@node A Quick Rundown of Docker Terminology
@section A Quick Rundown of Docker Terminology

@tindex Dockerfile
When working with Docker you start with a @code{Dockerfile} . Each line in this Dockerfile creates a @code{Layer}@comma{} and Docker will try to be smart about caching these layers so that if you re-use your Dockerfile it will start from the last layer that is the same as one it has previously built.

@pindex Docker Hub
@cindex Docker Hub
Based on this Dockerfile you create an @code{Image}. This is a snapshot in time from when all the lines in the Dockerfile have been executed. You can version control and tag this image if you like@comma{} and it is this image that you will push to Docker hub @uref{https://hub.docker.com/}. If you are using a ready-made container from Docker hub@comma{} e.g. with a database or a web server installed@comma{} it is this image that will be downloaded and used as a layer in your own Docker image.

When you start your Container@comma{} it will use the image as a base and execute the @code{ENTRYPOINT} in the Dockerfile@comma{} which is the command and its parameters that should be used to start your microservice. You now have your running Container.

@example
                    (/-/---------\-\)
                     \  Docker hub /
                      -\         /-
                        ---------   
                            ^ 
                            |
                            v 
+--------------+     +--------------+     +--------------+
| Dockerfile   |     | Image        |     | Container    |
|              | --> |              | --> |              |
|              |     |              |     |              |
|              |     |              |     |              |
+--------------+     +--------------+     +--------------+
| ENTRYPOINT   |                                 ^
+------+-------+                                 |
       +-----------------------------------------+

@end example

@node Dockerfile
@section Dockerfile 

Typically@comma{} a Dockerfile will contain at least the following commands (Most of which can appear more than once):

@findex Docker FROM
@code{FROM} to specify which image to use as a base for the container. This can either be an image you have created yourself@comma{} or a ready-made image from Docker hub. Example: @code{FROM node:18-alpine} says that you want to use an image that has node.js version 18 installed on top of an Alpine Linux install (which is a lightweight Linux distribution@comma{} more than which you probably do not need).

@findex Docker RUN
@pindex nodemon
@code{RUN} @comma{} which has a whole range of different parameters ( @uref{https://docs.docker.com/engine/reference/builder/#run} )@comma{} but you would typically use this to add any programs that you need but which were not part of the original image. Example @code{RUN npm install -g nodemon} means that you wish to use npm (one of the package managers available for node.js) to install the program @code{nodemon}. Nodemon is used during development to restart a node.js application as soon as any of its files change.

@findex Docker EXPOSE
@code{EXPOSE} is used to flag network ports that can be of use outside of the container. Example: @code{EXPOSE 3000} flags port 3000 as being accessible.

@strong{Please note}: This only instructs Docker that this port @emph{can} be opened. You still have to explicitly tell Docker that you @emph{want} it open and mapped to a particular port on your host machine when starting your container.

@findex Docker WORKDIR
@code{WORKDIR} instructs Docker that when you start the container@comma{} this is the directory it should start in. Example @code{WORKDIR /app} to start in the @code{/app} directory.

@findex Docker COPY
@code{COPY} is used to move any files from your host machine into the image. You would for example use this to copy the code for your microservice into the container. You can also use it to copy a specific configuration etc. Example: @code{COPY . .} copies everything in the current directory on the host machine (probably the directory where your Dockerfile is located) into the current working directory inside the container (which you may have set with a previous @code{WORKDIR} command).

At this point you are in many cases done with what needs to be installed@comma{} what remains is perhaps to set a few environment variables@comma{} and instruct Docker how you want to start your microservice. If you are creating a node.js application@comma{} this is a good point to ensure that all node.js packages you depend on are also installed. Another RUN takes care of this: @code{RUN npm install}

@findex Docker ENV
Setting an environment variable so that node.js@comma{} when running@comma{} knows what debug output to focus on: @code{ENV DEBUG='qfapp:*'}.

@findex Docker ENTRYPOINT
@findex Docker CMD
@code{ENTRYPOINT} is typically the last line in your Dockerfile@comma{} and it specifies which command to run and which parameters to use for it. You can specify a @code{CMD} instead (or together with @code{ENTRYPOINT})@comma{} but it is generally recommended to try with only an @code{ENTRYPOINT} first@comma{} since this can be replaced even after the image is created (e.g. if you realise that you want to try out a different start command). The preferred format (which can be used for other command such as @code{RUN} too) is to use an array format for the command: @code{ENTRYPOINT ["npm"@comma{} "run"@comma{} "dev"]} .

@findex Docker VOLUME
@findex Docker USER
@findex Docker ADD
Other commands that you sometimes see are used to mount VOLUMEs@comma{} or set the USER@. Please see the full reference for the Dockerfile @uref{https://docs.docker.com/engine/reference/builder/}  for more information.

@example
FROM node:18-alpine
RUN npm install -g nodemon
EXPOSE 3000
WORKDIR /app
COPY . .
RUN npm install
ENV DEBUG='qfapp:*'
ENTRYPOINT ["npm"@comma{} "run"@comma{} "dev"]
@end example

@node Docker Commands
@section Docker Commands

Now that you have created your Dockerfile@comma{} the next step is to start using it.

@enumerate
@item
@anchor{Getting Help}Getting Help


@pindex man
@pindex info
For almost all commands in Unix@comma{} you have a few different ways to get help. Running the command with the parameter @samp{-h} @comma{} or @samp{--help} (note the two dashes) is a good start. For more detailed information@comma{} you use the command @samp{man}. Sometimes@comma{} you can get even more details if you use the command @samp{info}. Try the following commands:

@example
docker -h
docker --help
@end example

@pindex more
@pindex less
Wow@comma{} that was a long page. Let's pipe it through something that paginates it for us. We can use either @samp{more} or its more enabled version @samp{less} for this.

@example
docker -h | more 
docker --help | less
@end example

To see how much more information you can get@comma{} try the following commands (you can also learn more about @samp{man} by looking at @emph{its} man-page: @samp{man man}):

@example
man docker
info docker
@end example

Pressing the key @samp{h} while on a man-page gives you instructions on how to navigate the document.

@item
@anchor{Docker image and Image Management}Docker image and Image Management 


First@comma{} we need to use the Dockerfile and create an image. This image downloads everything that should be installed -- provisioned -- in order to run your application later. During this process@comma{} all commands in your Dockerfile will be executed@comma{} and the image will be stored ready for use. Note that once this step is complete@comma{} nothing is actually running. All you have is a chunk of disk space that is @emph{ready} to run.

@findex docker build
@findex docker image build
The command is @samp{docker build} @comma{} and we want to look in the current directory for the Dockerfile so we add a period to the command. However@comma{} if we do this we get an image which has no name and only an unwieldy image id to work with. So let's tag the image as we create it with the @samp{-t <tagname>} flag.

@example
docker build -t myfirstimage .
@end example

@findex docker image
@findex docker image ls
Did it work? Let's view all images:
@example
docker image ls
@end example

The output should be something like:

@verbatim
REPOSITORY                    TAG         IMAGE ID       CREATED         SIZE
myfirstimage                  latest      270970b0971b   5 seconds ago   232MB
@end verbatim

What else can we do? Try @samp{docker image --help} . Apparently we can (among other things) build@comma{} list@comma{} inspect@comma{} remove@comma{} and tag images with this command. @samp{docker build} is@comma{} in fact@comma{} an alias for @samp{docker image build}.

@findex docker image rm
Once you are done (not yet)@comma{} you may want to clean up after yourself. Each image has a name listed under REPOSITORY@comma{} a tag listed under TAG@comma{} and an IMAGE ID@. If there is only one image with that name@comma{} you can run @samp{docker image rm myfirstimage}. If you have several images with the same name (try creating one more with @samp{docker build -t myfirstimage:second .})@comma{} you will need to qualify the name with the specific TAG you want to remove. If all else fail (e.g. if you forgot to give the image a name in the first place)@comma{} you can use the IMAGE ID to uniquely identify your image.

@item
@anchor{Docker run and Container Management}Docker run and Container Management


@findex docker run
@findex docker container run
So@comma{} let's get this image to actually run as a container@comma{} using the command @samp{docker run}. We need a few parameters for this to work as we want it. Some of the more commonly used parameters are listed in Table @ref{tab-docker-run-parameters}

@float Table,tab-docker-run-parameters
