@end float

The most common ones you will see are the detach and publish flags@comma{} and you will often see these together: @samp{docker run -dp 8080:3000}@comma{} which will open port 8080 on your host into port 3000 inside the container.

When developing your application@comma{} it is useful to be able to view any console output it makes (e.g. if you use @samp{printf()}@comma{} @samp{cout}@comma{} @samp{console.log()}@comma{} or @samp{(print)} inside your application). @samp{docker run -it} enables this@comma{} and also enables you to give commands into your running application if it is reading from standard input.

@findex docker container
@findex docker container ps
@findex docker container ls
@findex docker ps
You can view running containers with @samp{docker container ps}@comma{} @samp{docker container ls}@comma{} or just @samp{docker ps} :

@verbatim
CONTAINER ID   IMAGE     COMMAND         CREATED         STATUS         PORTS      NAMES
988c2fa79431   tst       ``npm run dev''   7 seconds ago   Up 6 seconds   3000/tcp   pensive@math{_hugle}
@end verbatim

@findex docker container stop
@findex docker container start
@findex docker container rm

the CONTAINER ID is used as a handle to do other things with the container. We can@comma{} for example@comma{} stop the container and later start it again with @samp{docker container stop <container-id>} and @samp{docker container start <container-id>}. If we stop the container we can also remove it completely (e.g. in order to update with a newer image): @samp{docker container rm <container-id>} (adding the ``force''-flag @samp{-f} to rm will first stop the container if it is running@comma{} saving you one command: @samp{docker rm -f <container-id>}).
@end enumerate

@node Not Building Microservices -- Infrastructure As A Service
@section Not Building Microservices -- Infrastructure As A Service 

@cindex Virtual Machine
@cindex IAAS Infrastructure As A Service
The alternative to running microservices would be to run a full-fledged virtual machine with its full operating system and everything. Maybe even a graphical user interface. Sometimes this is the better option@comma{} especially if an application consists of several collaborating processes that expect to co-exist on the same machine. We @emph{could} run these inside a docker container too@comma{} but this would sort of break the minimalistic view that one container should essentially only run a single command.

@cindex Hypervisor
In order to run virtual machines@comma{} you need a Hypervisor software that can act as a management layer between your host operating system and the guest operating system that you want to run in your virtual machine (Technically@comma{} I suppose that Docker/Podman should also be considered hypervisors). The virtual machine is supposed to emulate the hardware@comma{} so you are free to -- in theory -- run any operating system built for any platform. In practice@comma{} however@comma{} there are limitations to this.

@example
        +-----------------+ +-----------------+
        |   Guest OS      | |    Guest OS     |
        +-----------------+ +-----------------+
        +-----------------+ +-----------------+
        | Virtual Machine | | Virtual Machine |
        +-----------------+ +-----------------+
        +-------------------------------------+          
        |  Hypervisor                         |          
        +-------------------------------------+      
+----------------------------------------------------+
|          Host Operating System                     |
+----------------------------------------------------+
+----------------------------------------------------+
|          Computer Hardware                         |
+----------------------------------------------------+
@end example

@pindex Virtualbox
@pindex VMWare
@pindex Hyper-V
@pindex Parallells
Some common hypervisors include:
@itemize
@item
Virtualbox @uref{https://www.virtualbox.org/} 
@item
VMWare@comma{} with VMWare Fusion for Mac @uref{https://www.vmware.com/products/fusion.html}@comma{} and VMWare Workstation for Windows and Linux @uref{https://www.vmware.com/products/workstation-player.html}
@item
Parallells @uref{https://www.parallels.com/se/}  if you are using a Mac
@item
Hyper-V @uref{https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/virtualization/hyper-v-on-windows/} if you are using Windows
@end itemize

Of these@comma{} Virtualbox is free for use and available on most operating systems and mostly works without problems. It is@comma{} however@comma{} limited to x86 and AMD64/Intel64 hardware architectures so if you are using e.g. a more recent Mac you will not be able to use it. In those cases@comma{} you @emph{can} make do with VMWare or Parallells@comma{} but each of them have their own unique set of error messages and challenges to overcome in order to work seamlessly.

@pindex Qemu
Qemu @uref{https://www.qemu.org/} is a more powerful and free alternative that actually does emulate the virtual hardware as well. So you can emulate an x86 architecture on top of a mac M1 machine@comma{} or vice versa. The installation and setup @emph{looks} more daunting than e.g. Virtualbox does@comma{} but it is really quite easy -- at least on a Linux machine. In my experience@comma{} Qemu is also much faster than a corresponding virtual machine in Virtualbox.

@node Provisioning your Virtual Machine
@section Provisioning your Virtual Machine

@cindex Provisioning
With a hypervisor such as above@comma{} you get a clean virtual machine@comma{} and it is up to you to install whatever operating system you want to run@comma{} and whatever software you want installed on top of this operating system. You @emph{can} do this manually@comma{} but if you remember the goals we set up in the Introduction (See @ref{introduction})@comma{} we want to do better than this. We wish help from the computer to create a repeatable and configuration managed deployment that can be used over and over again.

@pindex Vagrant
@tindex Vagrantfile
This is where software such as Vagrant @uref{https://www.vagrantup.com/} comes into play (Update 2026: Sadly@comma{} Vagrant is on the verge of being discontinued) . With Vagrant we create a file @samp{Vagrantfile} where we specify the size and type of virtual machines we want@comma{} the operating system we want on them@comma{} and any other software we want installed -- analogous to the @samp{Dockerfile} discussed earlier@comma{} but with much more details. It is important to note that Vagrant does not create any virtual machines itself@comma{} it is not a hypervisor. Instead@comma{} it interacts with other hypervisors such as Virtualbox@comma{} VMWare@comma{} Hyper-V@comma{} or Qemu and requests them to do the ``heavy lifting'' for it. The beauty of this is that you learn once how to work with Vagrant@comma{} and can use the same Vagrantfile to create your same virtual machine using any of the supported hypervisors and indeed even launch your virtual machine on a cloud provider.

Unlike Docker@comma{} a Vagrantfile can also specify several machine configurations in one@comma{} so you can orchestrate your entire application at once instead of having to work separately with each component/microservice in your system setup. But this is another story which we will get to later (see @ref{Deployment}).

@node Communicating Microservices and REST APIs
@section Communicating Microservices and REST APIs 

@cindex Network Socket
@cindex RPC Remote Procedure Call
With microservices we are tearing apart our monolith application into several smaller units. Where we previously could use a simple method call inside the same process@comma{} we must now reach for other tools. Since these smaller units need not run on the same (virtual) machine@comma{} we must also resort to communication means that are network-enabled. If we truly love pain@comma{} we may develop our own communication protocol and use simple sockets@comma{} and for some applications this may be the best way to establish a fast enough and secure enough communications means. One step up@comma{} we may rely on Remote Procedure Calls (which still use sockets but we do not need to see them).

@cindex HTTP protocol
@cindex JSON grammar
@cindex XML
What is more common today@comma{} however@comma{} is that we make use of ready-made components to turn our microservice in to a small web server. The HTTP protocol is already well established and provide support for most of the tasks we need (i.e.@comma{} GET@comma{} POST@comma{} PUT@comma{} PATCH@comma{} and DELETE). The payload of the communication protocol still need to be defined@comma{} of course@comma{} but much effort can be saved by sticking to existing grammars such as JSON or XML@.

@cindex REST API
This leads us to Representational State Transfer@comma{} or REST APIs for short. Key principles for a REST API are (source: @uref{https://www.ibm.com/topics/rest-apis} ):

@table @asis
@item Uniform Interface
All requests for the same resource should look the same. So we cannot embed state or cookies or user session or whatever in the address of the resource (but we can include such information in the actual request)
@item Client-Server Decoupling
Clients and Servers are independent from each other@comma{} all we need to know is the address of a requested resource. In fact@comma{} this is all that is ever available to us.
@item Statelessness
This one is interesting given the original description of the REST pattern as transitioning a service through a series of states. The key here is that the server does not keep track of the state for every client that is connected@comma{} this is instead made part of each client's requests.
@item Cacheability
Whenever possible@comma{} the same request should give the same answer (this may not be desired if@comma{} for example@comma{} content is being continuously generated@comma{} and in those cases the resource should flag itself as being not cacheable).
@end table

As already almost stated@comma{} over time we have mostly settled on using HTTP (or HTTPS) as the communications protocol and JSON or XML as the data protocol for our REST APIs. Because there are ready-made components for many programming languages today@comma{} it is easy to start listening for HTTP calls and to parse JSON data. Creating HTTP calls is equally easy@comma{} and so we have an easy mechanism for providing and using a service that works across system borders.

@cindex Layered Architecture Style
The downside is that we break the seamlessness of our application. Ideally (and this was once the sales pitch for remote procedure calls)@comma{} we want to make all function/method calls in the same way throughout the entire application@comma{} no matter the physical distribution of parts of the application to different servers. In fact@comma{} the first thing we do when we create a microservice is probably to define a @emph{Layered} architecture style where the top layer deals with all the HTTP/JSON complexities@comma{} so that the rest of the application can be an as pure representation as possible of the domain concepts. Likewise@comma{} when we develop clients that need to make REST calls to another service@comma{} we will probably create a layer that marshal regular function calls into JSON/HTTP requests and unmarshall the response into a return value that knows how to behave like a proper object.

@node Other communication means
@section Other communication means

Do note that the required statelessness of a REST service means that the communication should only ever be one-directional. A well behaved service dutifully waits until someone makes an HTTP call@comma{} responds@comma{} and then goes back to waiting for the next call. If we expect the service to call back at a later stage (perhaps when a long-running job is done@comma{} or when a monitored resource becomes available@comma{} or when a specific user comes online@comma{} etc.)@comma{} there should not be any way for the service to even know about the clients. And yet sometimes we need this@comma{} which means we sometimes need to break the simpleness of our REST service.

@cindex Dependency Injection
First@comma{} we may need some mental acrobatics to decide that a request is not done until we have called back to the originator with results. If the initial request contains the return address@comma{} we can pretend that we are still working from within the same request and we are still (please click your heels three times and believe very firmly) working within the conceptual model of the REST architecture pattern.

One challenge is that we may not be able to spawn new threads inside our application@comma{} e.g. if the programming language or paradigm does not support threads. Thus@comma{} if the client makes a synchronous call@comma{} our hands are tied and we must deal with the execution thread we have been given to us@comma{} process the request@comma{} and return the answer. Ideally@comma{} we want something like:

@enumerate
@item
A call is made to our REST service together with a callData object.
@item
We store the callData object.
@item
We prepare a response to the REST call and write this to the open network socket.
@item
We close the network socket but DO NOT return from our method just yet.
@item
Now@comma{} we can start processing the callData object and figure out what they really wanted us to do.
@item
Once we have prepared our more detailed response@comma{} we look in the callData object and locate the address we should use for our return call.
@item
@emph{We} make a REST call to the client and drop off our carefully prepared response.
@item
And now@comma{} finally@comma{} we can return from our method inside the REST service.
@end enumerate

@dots{} Assuming@comma{} of course@comma{} that the client is accessible through layers and layers of firewalls and NAT:ed networks. I think you get the idea that this is not precisely a neat and tidy way of programming. Nor is it easily supported by common web frameworks@comma{} which usually expect steps 3 and 4 to be done by returning from our method@comma{} and thus giving back the execution thread.

Our next option is to coerce the client to make an asynchronous call to us. This is tricky using the standard HTTP api. A message queue is a better alternative@comma{} and is something which we can (and will have to) do by introducing a separate microservice in charge of the message queues. The workflow is now:

@enumerate
@item
The client drops off a message in the message queue and returns.
@item
The REST service gets notified that there is a message to process@comma{} or simply polls at regular time intervals.
@item
The REST service collects the next message to process.
@item
The REST service process the message and prepares a response.
@item
The REST service looks up the return address in the message@comma{} and returns the response accordingly.
@end enumerate

We're getting there. Now all we need to do is decide on how the REST service should contact the client. As before@comma{} the client can provide a REST api of their own@comma{} in order to receive the return call. Or@comma{} since we now have a microservice for managing message queues in place@comma{} we can use this:

@enumerate
@item
The client opens up a return queue
@item
The client drops off a message (containing@comma{} among other things@comma{} the address to the return queue) in the outgoing message queue and returns.
@item
The client checks the return queue until a message arrives.
@end enumerate

Having established that this is indeed possible@comma{} the question remains: Is this a good idea? Please discuss this in small groups.

@pindex Redis
@tindex MQTT
The message queue may be implemented in many ways. We may@comma{} for example@comma{} use a dedicated table in our database (to which our entire system is connected anyway). We may dedicate a separate microservice running e.g. Redis @uref{https://redis.io/}@comma{} and use an implementation of a message queue that connects to this microservice. We may even wish to go formal and pick a service that implements the MQTT protocol (Message Queue Telemetry Transport protocol). But that's a story for another day.

