@node Introducing the QuoteFinder application
@section Introducing the QuoteFinder application

@pindex QuoteFinder Version1
@pindex QFStandalone

@emph{QuoteFinder} is the application we are going to use for testing purposes. This application consist of a web site where you can search for quotes in texts. @emph{Version 1}@comma{} which we are starting with@comma{} simply searches for the entire string in its entirety. In @emph{Version 2} and @emph{Version 3}@comma{} this basic search algorithm is replaced by one that allows for a more advanced search where the words may appear near each other if not directly together. We will initially stick to version 1@comma{} as it is mostly a @emph{standalone} version. We will later come back to version 1 when we explore other concepts. In this part of the tutorial we are going to@comma{} step by step@comma{} install and run QuoteFinder version 1 in different ways@comma{} but first let us explore the application a bit.


@pindex node.js
@pindex Express
@pindex MongoDB
@pindex socket.io
QuoteFinder Version 1 has the following characteristica:
@itemize
@item
it is written in node.js @uref{https://nodejs.org/}
@item
it is an Express web app @uref{http://expressjs.com/}
@item
it also uses socket.io @uref{https://socket.io/} to communicate between the web client and the express server
@item
it uses a MongoDB database @uref{https://www.mongodb.com/}
@end itemize

@cindex Project Gutenberg
Once started@comma{} there are three landing pages:
@itemize
@item
@code{/} This is the start page@comma{} where you enter text to search for.
@item
@code{/add} From the @code{/add} page you can add new texts to the database. For adding texts@comma{} we recommend Project Gutenberg @uref{https://www.gutenberg.org/} . Indeed@comma{} if you leave the text fields empty@comma{} you will add a plaintext copy of Leo Tolstoy's @emph{War and Peace} to your database.
@item
@code{/list} Last but not least@comma{} @code{/list} allows you to view a list of currently added texts.
@end itemize

Being an Express app@comma{} this is all controlled by so called routes@comma{} which are set up in the file @code{src/index.js}:

@example
var router = express.Router();
router.get('/'@comma{} startPage);
router.get('/add'@comma{} addTextPage);
router.get('/list'@comma{} listTextsPage);
app.use('/'@comma{} router);
@end example

The second argument for each of the @code{router.get()} calls is a reference to a function@comma{} also found in @code{index.js} . These are fairly small@comma{} and mostly rely on jade/pug to render a page back to the user. The @code{listTextsPage()} is slightly longer since it first creates a new @code{TextManager} object (more on this class later) to find all the available texts from the database.

@example
function startPage(req@comma{} res) @{
    console.log('Loading start page from: ' + req.hostname);
    return res.render('index'@comma{} serverIDMessage);
@}

function addTextPage(req@comma{} res) @{
    console.log('Serving the "Add New Text" page...');
    return res.render('addText');
@}

function listTextsPage(req@comma{} res) @{
    console.log('Listing available texts.');
    let tm = new TextManager();
    return tm.connect()
        .then( tm.listTexts )
        .then(texts => res.render('index'@comma{} @{textList: texts@comma{}
                                                ...serverIDMessage@}) );
@}
@end example

@pindex Jade
@pindex Pug
@findex JavaScript Promise
Jade/Pug is out of scope for this brief walk-through@comma{} but the templates used to render the pages can be found in the directory @code{/src/views} . One more thing to point out here is the use of @emph{Promises} rather than the usual callback hell that you often end up in with JavaScript (the @code{then()} - daisy-chain is a clue that Promises are being used).

The rendered web page connects back to the express server using @emph{socket.io} @comma{} so when a button is pressed in the web browser@comma{} this does not generate a new HTTP call back@comma{} but rather just a message on an already open communications socket. This seamlessly extends the event-driven architecture of node.js (with @code{EventEmitter.emit()} and @code{EventEmitter.on()} ) to also work across system boundaries to the web browser. In the directory @code{/src/public/js} you will find the two client-side javascript files that takes care of this.

This design decision was taken so that the web page could be rendered in pieces. For example@comma{} when a search is being executed@comma{} partial results can be added to the page while still waiting for more complex calculations. It will@comma{} however@comma{} cause problems for us down the line@comma{} so please remember that you have been forewarned.

Because the socket object is required in order to send results back to the web client@comma{} the methods that require this (i.e. @code{searchTexts()} and @code{addText()} ) are declared once for each connection. That is also when the server-side socket is connected to pass on messages from the client to these two methods:

@example
socket.on('search'@comma{} searchTexts );
socket.on('addText'@comma{} addText)    
socket.on('disconnect'@comma{} () => @{ console.log('user disconnected'); @});
@end example

@dots{} And that pretty much takes care of @code{index.js}@comma{} with some excursions into views and client side code. The remainder is mostly Express boilerplate code to deal with simple error handling and to actually start listening for connections.

The other component needed is the TextManager class@comma{} which is responsible for connecting to MongoDB@comma{} and searching@comma{} adding@comma{} and listing texts. The connection can be done in two different ways@comma{} but for now we will only be using the second alternative where (optionally) an environment variable @code{TEXTSTORE_HOST} decides the IP-address for the MongoDB server. If left unspecified@comma{} TextManager assumes localhost (127.0.0.1).

A conceptual view of the application:

@example
+----------------------+                    +--------------------+---------------+
| Web Client           |<------------------>| QuoteFinder        | Jade/Pug      |
+----------------------+   Socket.io        |                    | Page Rendering|
                           and              +--------------------+---------------+
                           HTTP             | TextManager        |         
                                            |                    |
                                            +---------+----------+
                                                      |      
                                            +---------+----------+
                                            | MongoDB Database   |
                                            +--------------------+
@end example

