@node Installing and Running QFStandalone
@section Installing and Running QFStandalone

@enumerate
@item
@anchor{Docker}Docker


The next step is to microservice the app. QFStandalone is still simple enough so that it will be run as a single microservice. However@comma{} we want to run the database as a separate microservice. If nothing else@comma{} this will mean that any books that we add to the database will remain when we rebuild the QFStandalone image.

This time@comma{} we are going to fetch the QuoteFinder app and pretend to do our development locally@comma{} so that we are only @emph{deploying} as a microservice.

@strong{Tasks}
0.[@@0] Open up a terminal as before@comma{} create a new sub-directory e.g. @code{QuoteFinder/Version1/docker} @comma{} and cd into it.

@enumerate
@item
Clone the QuoteFinder repository: @code{git clone https://github.com/mickesv/QuoteFinder/} @comma{}  extract Version1 by copying it @code{cp -r QuoteFinder/Containers/Version1 .}  @comma{} and cd into the directory @code{cd Version1/QFStandalone}

@item
In this directory you will find a @code{Dockerfile} @comma{} with instructions for docker on how to create a docker image from this directory (see also @ref{Dockerfile} ).
@end enumerate

@example
FROM node:18-alpine
RUN npm install -g nodemon
EXPOSE 3000
WORKDIR /app
COPY . .
RUN npm install
ENV DEBUG='qfapp:*'
ENTRYPOINT ["npm"@comma{} "run"@comma{} "dev"]
@end example

Please take a moment to study this file and make sure you understand what each line does. Pay particular notice to the line @code{WORKDIR /app} and the @code{COPY . .} instructions. With these two instructions we set the working directory inside the image to @code{/app} and copy everything from the current working directory on the host into the image's working directory. After these two instructions@comma{} everything that the image requires to get going is essentially in place@comma{} and all that is needed is to install dependencies from the just now inserted @code{package.json}.

@enumerate 3
@item
Build the image: @code{docker build -t qfstandalone .} 
@item
Check that the image is indeed created: @code{docker image ls} 
@item
For the sake of it@comma{} let's tag it as well: @code{docker tag qfstandalone:latest mickesv/qfstandalone:version1} (replace 'mickesv' with your username on Docker Hub). If you have an account on Docker Hub@comma{} you might also wish to push the image thither. Currently this has little meaning unless you wish to proudly distribute your own copy of QuoteFinder.
@end enumerate

At this stage we can actually start the container@comma{} but it will not yet be able to connect to your database (you don't even have a database image installed yet). So we'll continue setting up things a while longer before we actually launch the app:

@enumerate 6
@item
Download the @samp{mongo} image: @code{docker pull mongo}
@item
Create a virtual network@comma{} so that we might connect our two microservices: @code{docker network create qfstandalone-net}
@item
Start the MongoDB container. This incantation has a few commands built in@comma{} so let's break it down first:
@end enumerate

@example
docker run                  # Start a Container
-d                          # In detached mode (in the background)
--network qfstandalone-net  # Connect to the virtual network we just created
--network-alias textstore   # Make this container accessible
                            # on the network using this name
--name textstore            # Use this name when we access 
                            # the container with docker
mongo                       # Use this image as base for the container
@end example

In one line: @code{docker run -d --network qfstandalone-net --network-alias textstore --name textstore mongo}
@enumerate 9
@item
Now we are finally ready to start the QuoteFinder container. Again@comma{} let's break down the incantation first:
@end enumerate

@example
docker run                    # Start a container
-it                           # In interactive mode@comma{} and attach 
                              # a terminal so we can also type into it
--network qfstandalone-net    # Same virtual network
-e TEXTSTORE_HOST=textstore   # Set the environment variable to the 
                              # network alias of our MongoDB database
-w /app                       # Set the working directory inside the container
-v ./src:/app/src             # Attach the host directory ./src 
                              # to the guest under /app/src
--name qfstandalone           # Docker name
-p 8080:3000                  # Connect host port 8080 to 
                              # port 3000 in the container
mickesv/qfstandalone:version1 # Use this image (the tag we previously set)
@end example

One long string: @code{docker run -it --network qfstandalone-net -e TEXTSTORE_HOST=textstore -w /app -v ./src:/app/src --name qfstandalone -p 8080:3000 mickesv/qfstandalone:version1} 

@enumerate 10
@item
We can now test the app. Open a web browser to @uref{http://localhost:8080/add} and click the 'Add' button to add a book to your database. Your terminal window where the app is running will print some info@comma{} hopefully ending with @emph{Text added.} If not@comma{} make note of any error messages printed.

@item
Go to @uref{http://localhost:8080/} and search for something@comma{} e.g. 'prince'. This will give a list of hits. Remember to keep an eye on the output in the terminal.

@item
Let us now test editing the app. On your host machine@comma{} open up the file @code{src/index.js} @comma{} find the function @samp{startPage()}@comma{} replace the return line with @samp{return listTextsPage(req@comma{} res);} and save. Notice that the app is reloaded directly@comma{} and when you reload the start page in your web browser you will now see a list of available texts.
@end enumerate

The reason why this works so much more seamlessly compared to the full-virtual-machine approach we tried before is because we are mounting the host directory into the guest with the @code{-v ./src:/app/src} flag. In effect@comma{} we are replacing the entire @samp{src} directory inside the container with whatever we have saved locally@comma{} thus overloading whatever we put in there when we created the image. If we remove the @samp{-v} flag@comma{} we will revert back to whatever was in there when we created the image. As before@comma{} @code{nodemon} restarts for us if it detects that a file has been changed.

If you look in the @samp{Version1/QFStandalone} directory on your host computer@comma{} you will notice that it only contains the code that you yourself have created (or@comma{} <ahem!>@comma{} yours truly :-) . There is no directory @code{node_modules} @comma{} and there is no @code{package-lock.json}. These are created @emph{inside} the container@comma{} and actually already inside the image. This is nice@comma{} you do not clutter your own disk with lots of node.js cruft (you actually do not even need to have node.js installed locally)@comma{} but it does mean that you need to rebuild the image when you want to add a package dependency to your @code{package.json}. This is a slight hassle@comma{} but as a consolation it does not happen very often once you have settled on an architecture for your system.
@enumerate 13
@item
Are we still running? Check with @code{docker ps} to see what containers are up and running.
@end enumerate

@strong{Summary}
We have now:
@enumerate
@item
Cloned the QuoteFinder application to our local machine
@item
Created a Dockerfile and a Docker image for Version1/QFStandalone.
@item
Downloaded a MongoDB docker image
@item
Created a virtual network
@item
Started a MongoDB database as a microservice
@item
Started Version1/QFStandalone as a microservice
@item
Connected Version1/QFStandalone with MongoDB using our virtual network and started the app so that we may test it.
@item
Edited a file locally and see the running app inside the container restart to reflect the updated version.
@end enumerate

Not bad for a first try@comma{} eh?

@strong{Cleanup}
@example
docker rm -f textstore qfstandalone
docker network rm qfstandalone-net
docker network prune -f
@end example

@strong{Remaining Pain Points}
