@node Provisioning
@section Provisioning

@cindex Provisioning
Whether we are setting up a physical computer@comma{} a virtual machine@comma{} or a microservice@comma{} we need to configure it and decide which software should be installed. This process is called @emph{Provisioning}. When we are installing a computer or a full virtual machine@comma{} it is possible to do this by logging on to the machine and installing software interactively. The downside to this is that we create a fragile system where any particular installation may break the system in new and creative ways. When this happens@comma{} our only available solution may be to tear down the entire machine and re-install it from scratch again.

As good engineers we of course maintain a logbook of everything we do to the machine@comma{} in what order@comma{} and any errors or warnings that we get during the installation process so that we may go back and analyse what went wrong and try a different approach next time.

As better engineers@comma{} we encode our installation into a @emph{script} that the computer can run for us. Eventually we are able to review the log output to see what went wrong and how to modify the installation script.

We have already mentioned two forms of provisioning; the one we made with Docker@comma{} and one using tools that scripts the provisioning of a full VM@. Let us examine the differences.

With Docker we started with an almost ready machine (@samp{FROM node:18-alpine}) @comma{} and the focus was to install @emph{anything else} that we needed and to further configure the microservice image. We also had to repeat some of this configuration on the command line when we started a container based on this image.

With a full VM provisioning we start with a relatively clean linux install (e.g. bento/ubuntu-20.04)@comma{} and then have to execute a series of shell commands in order to install the rest. We can not compare with the docker install straight off since we can install many tools inside the same VM@comma{} e.g. a MongoDB database@comma{} and we downloaded the entire QuoteFinder repository (not just its dependencies) inside the virtual machine@comma{} but typically there is a bit of @code{sudo apt-get update}@comma{} @code{sudo apt-get install -y}@comma{} some @code{echo} and @code{cd here/and/there} @comma{} and some copying of files with @samp{cp} . All the things you could expect to do interactively@comma{} but saved in a provisioning script to be able to run and re-run the provisioning at will. Technically@comma{} we @emph{can} do all of this in our Dockerfile too@comma{} but there is generally less need for it.

@pindex Ansible
@pindex Chef
@pindex Puppet
@findex YAML Ansible
If we do not wish to get our hands dirty with shell provisioning@comma{} we can use provisioning software instead. For example@comma{} Ansible @uref{https://docs.ansible.com/ansible/latest/getting_started/index.html} specifies the machines we have and their respective desired state in a collection of YAML-files@comma{} whereas Chef @uref{https://www.chef.io/} and Puppet @uref{https://www.puppet.com/} use their own homegrown language. While there are important differences (e.g. whether new configurations are @emph{pushed} to each machine or @emph{pulled} from a central repository@comma{} and whether the tools are open source or not)@comma{} the overall idea is to specify a desired state for each machine rather than specific shell instructions (potentially only valid for a specific linux dialect) for how to get to that state.

An added benefit to provisioning tools like this is that it is possible to update the desired state and let the provisioning tools transfer your deployment to the new state in a controlled manner.

@node Orchestration
@section Orchestration

@cindex Orchestration
As the number of services we wish to deploy grows (actually@comma{} as soon as it grows to more than one@comma{} e.g. an application and a database)@comma{} there is a need to manage the collection of deployments. An application may@comma{} for example@comma{} consist of several collaborating virtual machines@comma{} each requiring a specific provisioning and configuration of ports to forward@comma{} or there may be a desire to create more instances of the database and have some form of load balancing between them. This additional step is called @emph{Orchestration}. Vagrant does both; orchestration of which virtual machines to start ( for example@comma{} @code{config.vm.define "app"} creates one machine named ``app'')@comma{} and the provisioning of them ( @samp{config.vm.provision "shell"} ). This is practical for smaller setups@comma{} but the desire to @emph{separate concerns} means that it is soon desirable to delegate the provisioning to a provisioning tool such as Ansible and leave the Vagrantfile responsible only for the orchestration.

With tools such as Vagrant@comma{} adding more machines is as easy as adding additional blocks of @samp{config.vm.define}. For each defined virtual machine@comma{} the network alias@comma{} preferred IP address@comma{} and forwarded ports can be configured. Additional provisioning can also be added for each machine@comma{} but I would advise against this: it is better to rely on your designated provisioning tool for @emph{all} provisioning@comma{} and only include as little as is necessary to bootstrap this provisioning tool in your Vagrantfile. Separation of Concerns for the win!

@node Docker Compose
@section Docker Compose

@pindex Docker Compose
Docker's focus is on provisioning a single image@comma{} which is then used as a template to start one or more containers. As noticed@comma{} already with two containers the command line incantations to set up a virtual network and hook up the microservices to this becomes quite a mouthful. The next step up is to define all the infrastructure in a separate file@comma{} and use @samp{docker compose} to compose your application of several microservices@comma{} storage volumes@comma{} networks@comma{} etc.

@findex YAML Docker Compose
Docker Compose uses a YAML file to define the services@comma{} networks@comma{} volumes@comma{} etc. that should be configured and started. A Docker compose file for the previous Version 1 of the QuoteFinder app can@comma{} for example@comma{} look as follows:

@example
version: "3.8"
services:
  app:
    image: qfstandalone
    ports:
      - 8080:3000
    volumes:
      - ./Containers/Version1/QFStandalone/src:/app/src
    environment:
      TEXTSTORE_HOST: textstore
  textstore:
    image: mongo
    command: --quiet --syslog
    expose:
      - "27017"
@end example

And is started with @samp{docker compose -f docker-compose-v1.yml up}. I am not a fan of languages where white-space has a semantic meaning@comma{} but with YAML we are unfortunately forced to accept this. Going through the example above@comma{} we see that we define two services@comma{} @samp{app} and @samp{textstore}@comma{} each based on a separate docker image (qfstandalone and mongo@comma{} respectively). Unless otherwise specified@comma{} MongoDB is quite chatty so the @samp{command:} line is basically telling it to shut up and log things using the system logger instead. Do note that the network is implicitly defined@comma{} which is a relief.

@node Cloud Orchestration
@section Cloud Orchestration

So now you have your docker compose-file and are able to deploy any number of machines or services locally. The next step up is to deploy to a cloud provider instead. Some tools have has rudimentary support for this by allowing the definition of several different @samp{provider} blocks in the configuration@comma{} and determine at startup time whither to deploy@comma{} e.g.: @samp{vagrant up --provider <some provider>} . Docker compose@comma{} on the contrary@comma{} does not easily support this. 

@pindex Terraform
@pindex Kubernetes
What if you want to deploy to several providers in one go? What if you want to first deploy a small set of servers@comma{} and then declaratively extend this set as the load increases? Terraform @uref{https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform} is one example of a tool for this. If your application is constructed only with microservices@comma{} then Kubernetes @uref{https://kubernetes.io/} is the docker-equivalent. As far as I can tell@comma{} these two tools solve the same problem but for virtual machines versus microservices. Eventually@comma{} we will foray into Kubernetes with the QuoteFinder app@comma{} but not quite yet.

@pindex SaltStack
@pindex Hadoop
Yet one more alternative worth mentioning is Salt and its SaltStack @uref{http://saltstack.com/} . In many ways@comma{} Salt is a combination of a provisioning tool and an orchestration tool. Once a salt infrastructure is set up (with the ever so cool and hip names @samp{salt-master} and @samp{salt-minions} )@comma{} one distributes jobs across this infrastructure. These jobs may be to query a minion for some state@comma{} or to run a specific (UNIX) command on a particular minion. This also means that you can distribute any software to the minions so you can use and re-use your salt infrastructure over and over again with new jobs. In that sense@comma{} it is closer to parallel computing platforms such as Hadoop@comma{} or even Lambda Functions on e.g Azure or Amazon Web Services.

