@node Application Design and Development
@chapter Application Design and Development

@menu
* Principles of Microservice Architectures::
* Cloud Architecture Patterns : Cloud Architecture Patterns. 
* Introduction to YAML and Docker-Compose.yml: Introduction to YAML and Docker-Composeyml. 
@end menu

@node Principles of Microservice Architectures
@section Principles of Microservice Architectures

@cindex Scalability
@cindex Maintainability
The key quality requirement for cloud architecture patterns is @emph{Scalability}. This is a bit unusual@comma{} since most modularisation efforts rather focus on @emph{Maintainability}. Digging deeper@comma{} the goal is of course always to create a maintainably scalable solution@comma{} i.e. where scalability is built in and therefore any maintainability task that deals with scaling the application should be easy to do.

@cindex Vertical Scaling
@cindex Horizontal Scaling
Trivially@comma{} an application can scale in two directions@comma{} i.e. @emph{vertically} by adding more resources within each node (e.g. faster CPU's@comma{} more memory@comma{} bigger disks)@comma{} or @emph{horizontally} by adding more nodes. One refers to this as @emph{vertically scaling up}@comma{} and @emph{horizontally scaling out}. The goals of both these types of scalability is to support more concurrent users or jobs@comma{} or to decrease response times.

@cindex Reliability
@cindex Extensibility
Using the same scalability mechanisms@comma{} one can also increase @emph{reliability} of an application@comma{} e.g. by adding redundant nodes and a load balancer. Moreover@comma{} and this becomes more prevalent in microservice architectures@comma{} we can create an @emph{extensible} architecture where it is easy to add new functionality as standalone nodes in a microservice network.

B Wilder@comma{} @emph{Cloud Architecture Patterns}@comma{} O'Reilly@comma{} 2012. ISBN: 978-1-449-31977-9@comma{} defines some boundaries for cloud applications@comma{} that define the types of solutions one is able to imagine. Specifically@comma{} they enumerate:

@cindex Elastic Scaling
@cindex Metered Billing
@cindex IAC Infrastructure As Code
@table @asis
@item (The illusion of) Infinite Resources
Meaning@comma{} horizontal scaling is easy and preferred. It also means that there is no upper limit to how many services you can use to deploy in your application.
@item Elastic Scaling
When more resources are needed@comma{} they are available@comma{} but equally important is that you are able to release resources when they are no longer needed.
@item Metered Billing
This is the term that Rosenberg & Mateos@comma{} @emph{The cloud at your service}. Manning Publications Co. 2010. uses. You only pay for the resources that are currently being used. Again@comma{} the idea is to lull you into thinking that resources are infinite and infinitely cheap.
@item Automation
This scaling up and down is managed through automated tools@comma{} where the currently desired platform is programmatically expressed (Infrastructure as Code). There is no need for manual intervention by a platform engineer e.g. to make new resources available@comma{} or for new contracts to be negotiated for every change in the required cloud resources.
@end table

As a developer of a cloud-native application@comma{} these principles are in fact more or less sufficient. The knowledge that new resources are always available and under the control of the application itself@comma{} as and when needed means that an application can be developed as a collection of standalone units. For the @emph{cloud provider}@comma{} however@comma{} a few more principles are desirable to make this an economically viable business model:

@cindex Pooled Resources
@cindex Multitenancy
@cindex Virtualisation
@cindex Commodity Hardware
@table @asis
@item Multitenancy@comma{} or Pooled Resources
There may be any number of virtual resources running on each physical hardware unit@comma{} and any single user of a cloud service or of cloud infrastructure is not aware of who else may be sharing the physical platform at any given point. Each resource has its own CPU@comma{} memory@comma{} and disk@comma{} and is not aware of what else may be running next to it (or what they are paying for).
@item Virtualisation
In order to support the above@comma{} virtualisation of resources is a required technical solution. Without virtualisation@comma{} neither multitenancy@comma{} automation@comma{} nor elastic scaling@comma{} would be possible.
@item Commodity Hardware
To further keep the costs down for the cloud provider@comma{} it is desirable to avoid any specialised hardware. For example the CPUs@comma{} memory units@comma{} and hard drives should be as close to off-the-shelf units as possible@comma{} that could just as well be installed in a desktop computer.
@end table

Where the first group of principles @emph{enabled} cloud native applications@comma{} these additional principles @emph{restrict} the solutions. Critically@comma{} a cloud application can never be sure that a specific resource is available at a specific point in time. It might@comma{} for example@comma{} be in the process of migrating to a new hardware unit to make room for another customer's request for a bigger resource@comma{} or the hard disk may fail. As usual (to use a Swedish expression)@comma{} what you gain on the carousel@comma{} you loose on the swings. The cost per time unit for a specific cloud resource may be low@comma{} but this is somewhat balanced by the cost of the development time to create a fault-tolerant application.

@cindex Platform Service Ecosystem
@table @asis
@item Platform Service Ecosystem
This is one last piece of the puzzle for a cloud native application. A cloud solution requires more than just virtual CPU power. For example@comma{} data storage@comma{} a message bus@comma{} network facilities@comma{} monitoring facilities@comma{} the ability to move the application closer to its users@comma{} etc.@comma{} may also be required for a complete cloud application. Different cloud providers may provide a more or less rich ecosystem of platform services. When selecting between different cloud providers@comma{} these extra services may and should factor in the decision.
@end table

@node Cloud Architecture Patterns
@section Cloud Architecture Patterns 

B Wilder@comma{} @emph{Cloud Architecture Patterns}@comma{} O'Reilly@comma{} 2012 is a good starting point for the basic structures available to create scalable software architecture solutions for cloud native applications. Below is a brief summary of some of the patterns in this book; for details I encourage you to read the book yourself.


@strong{Horizontally Scaling Compute Pattern} and the @strong{Auto-Scaling Pattern}
Together@comma{} these two patterns suggest to prefer horizontal scaling and to use automated tools to request this scaling. When using horizontal scaling@comma{} each component in the application can more or less be single-threaded and should be kept as simple as possible. As I'm sure have been hammered into you by now@comma{} any time you are forced to write multi-threaded code an angel loses their wings.

@example
             @comma{}        @comma{}
            /(        )`
            \ \___   / |
            /- _  `-/  '
           (/\/ \ \   /\
           / /   | `    \
           O O   ) /    |
           `-^--'`<     '
          (_.)  _  )   /
           `.___/`    /
             `-----' /
<----.     __ / __   \
<----|====O)))==) \) /====
<----'    `--' `.__@comma{}' \
             |        |
              \       /
         ______( (_  / \______
       @comma{}'  @comma{}-----'   |        \
       `--@{__________)        \/
@end example

Challenges with horizontal scaling includes the need to be absolutely sure about the billing model the cloud provider uses@comma{} how to deal with session state@comma{} and how to update the nodes with a minimum of application downtime.


@strong{Queue-Centric Workflow Pattern}
This pattern is used to make the nodes more loosely connected. Without this pattern@comma{} when one node wishes to communicate something to another node (For example@comma{} a control message or a job request)@comma{} it first needs to find the right instance of the other node@comma{} and then wait for it to become available to receive the message. Meanwhile@comma{} the first node is also unavailable for new requests.

With the queue pattern in place@comma{} rather than going directly to the other node@comma{} the message is placed in a queue@comma{} and the first node can go back to being available. When one instance of the receiving node type becomes available@comma{} it will collect the messages in turn from the message queue and act upon them. This pattern is usually not relevant for read-only requests@comma{} since it is desirable to keep these synchronous. Rather@comma{} it is used when a job can be launched asynchronously and the calling node need not wait for the job to finish.

The actual queue can be implemented in many ways@comma{} either through a message bus provided by the cloud platform@comma{} a dedicated MQTT @uref{https://mqtt.org/} solution running on a separate node@comma{} or using some storage resource already available to all nodes (e.g. a shared filesystem or a database).

The queue should be more reliable than the nodes acting on the data so that no messages are lost@comma{} and it should be possible to re-process the same message multiple times with the same business result.


@cindex MapReduce
@strong{MapReduce Pattern}
This gives me a chance to write about my favourite programming language@comma{} @emph{lisp}@comma{} where this pattern is an intrinsic part of any data processing. Surprisingly@comma{} it reappears in more modern programming languages as well@comma{} such as JavaScript and Java (with the introduction of the Streams API). The underlying principle of this programming pattern is to transform a list into another list by applying a function to each element in the list@comma{} i.e. a @emph{map} from one domain to another. Multiple transformations can be daisy-chained through a series of @emph{maps}@comma{} and often this is sufficient to process the data as required. Sometimes@comma{} something needs to be computed across an entire list@comma{} i.e. @emph{reduce} the list down to for example a scalar. This is done by applying a function together with an accumulator variable to each element in the list.

@example
           map                 reduce
(a b c)  -------> (a' b' c') ----------> X
@end example

As it happens@comma{} this programming pattern is eminently suitable for scaling. In the extreme case@comma{} each application of the map function can be distributed to a separate node. Since each element in the list and the application of the map function on that element is independent from all other elements@comma{} they can be executed asynchronously@comma{} and the results collected and collated into the modified list. The reduce function can be trickier to distribute since there may be a dependency on that elements are reduced in a specific order. However@comma{} with some clever use of the right data structures (in particular the @emph{dictionary} data structure)@comma{} it is possible to create parallelisable versions of reduce functions as well.

When parallelising this programming pattern e.g. in a cloud native application@comma{} important aspects to consider is to ensure that each element is truly independent of all other elements@comma{} and that the map function can operate on each element individually. Additional map/reduce steps may be necessary in order to preprocess the data to ensure this. Scalability-wise@comma{} there is an overhead cost for a new computing node@comma{} since data need to be pre-processed and distributed to the node and the results need to be collected and collated@comma{} so it is important to fine-tune the number of jobs assigned to each computing node for optimal performance.


@strong{Node Failure} and @strong{Busy Signal} patterns
The fundamental programming principle in a cloud native application is that @emph{All application compute nodes should be ready to fail at any time.} As a corollary to this@comma{} all application compute nodes must be prepared that any other node is about to fail at any time.

Protecting from sudden and unplanned node failures@comma{} data that should be persistent should be kept in a persistent storage@comma{} jobs that have not been completed should remain in the job queue@comma{} and one might consider to have a certain number of redundant nodes that can pick up the slack when a node fails.

Nodes subjected to a planned failure (either planned by the cloud provider or by the application itself) have the luxury of both being able to complete what they are currently working on@comma{} gracefully removing themselves from accepting new jobs@comma{} and to alert e.g. load balancers that they are about to become unavailable.

From the calling side (be it the end user or another node inside your application)@comma{} it is important to maintain an updated list of available nodes@comma{} or delegate this to a load balancer or a worker queue. Calls between nodes should be programmed with a timeout to enable switching to an alternative strategy@comma{} should a node fail to reply. When a call times out@comma{} the options are to retry@comma{} wait a while and then retry@comma{} or switch to a backup node and retry. The 'retry' strategy is often sufficient if there is a load balancer in place@comma{} since the new call is likely to end up on a new node anyway.

When and whether to alert the load balancer that a node might be unavailable is another discussion.

Sometimes@comma{} a node is not completely down@comma{} it is just unable to process the request right now. For example@comma{} it did not get all of the message@comma{} some other component it relies on was temporarily unavailable@comma{} or it was busy doing some maintenance work. In that case@comma{} the node should return a ``busy signal''@comma{} for example a @samp{503 service unavailable} return on a http request. Good clients recognise this busy signal and have strategies for dealing with this. Usually@comma{} a retry is sufficient@comma{} or maybe first wait a while and then retry.


@strong{Colocate Pattern} and @strong{Multisite Deployment Pattern}
Nodes that work closely together should also be located as close to each other as is possible. Later on@comma{} you will learn how tools such as Kubernetes have built this into their microservices deployment architecture.

This principle extends to the end users. By deploying an application to many data centers across the world@comma{} users can be routed to the instance running closest to them@comma{} thereby reducing network latency. As it happens@comma{} this also enables redundancy in that if one data center fails@comma{} the work can be handed over to one of the application instances running on a different data center.

There is an overhead and a cost in running multiple instances of an application -- even when using the cloud provider's tools for moving the application around the world. Unless there is a tangible improvement in user experience@comma{} it may not be worth it to implement the multisite deployment pattern.

Another consideration is whether your deployments across the globe should shrink and grow over the course of a single day@comma{} to accommodate for the fact that users in a particular region are more or less active during a day (e.g. more activity in the early evenings and less at night).


@strong{Content Delivery Network (CDN)} and @strong{Valet Key} patterns
Building a RESTful microservice usually means starting with some web framework in your programming environment (e.g. an express application in node.js). This means that you have an adequate or possibly decent web server that is good at executing the application code@comma{} but may not be great as a web server. If your application is serving a lot of static contents@comma{} then it may not be able to keep up with the dynamic requests that will execute your application code.

The solution is to include a dedicated web server in your solution that is really good and optimised for serving static content. Either access this web server directly@comma{} or through dedicated nodes across the globe whose purpose is to cache oft-requested contents closer to the end-user. This network is solely focused on delivering contents and not bothered with anything else in your application. Specifically@comma{} the CDN does not deal with dynamic contents.

Sometimes (oftentimes) the delivered content should only be available to the one user that requested it (or at least a limited list of users)@comma{} or it should only be available for a specific period of time. That's where the Valet Key pattern comes in. When a user requests access to some content through your application@comma{} a unique URL is generated for this particular user@comma{} and the CDN server is instructed to make the requested resource available on that URL@. This URL may also be set up to only work for a limited time. Moreover@comma{} the URL may be used to also upload contents instead of just downloading or streaming.

If a really secure solution is desired@comma{} the URL should only be valid for a limited time and from a specific IP address. Furthermore@comma{} the contents can be encrypted so that the user must also have access to the decryption key.


@strong{Summary}
The architecture patterns for cloud native applications are centered around the ability to scale up and down as needed (This is perhaps not very surprising)@comma{} but the preference is to scale horizontally. Since the current state of the application (e.g. the number of available nodes of any particular type) can change at a moments notice@comma{} robust communication pathways using e.g. message queues are required. To some extent@comma{} this defines the execution view of the system's architecture@comma{} but with this definition comes a few limitations. Accepting that nodes can fail or be taken offline at any time means that applications need to be built with this in mind. Limitations in network speeds and a desire to make the best use of available computing resources influence where and how an application's services are deployed (e.g.@comma{} which parts of an application that are co-located or distributed closer to the customers) and indeed what constitutes a service in the first place (e.g. inserting a content delivery network in the application). In a more conceptual focus@comma{} the desire to enable scaling implies restructuring algorithms into more scaling-friendly forms@comma{} for example the MapReduce pattern. Finally@comma{} an open architecture across multiple nodes and servers requires that security and access control is considered throughout the application@comma{} for example using temporary and time-limited access keys.

@node Introduction to YAML and Docker-Composeyml
@section Introduction to YAML and Docker-Compose.yml

@tindex YAML
An increasing range of tools use YAML @uref{https://yaml.org/spec/} to describe configurations. As mentioned before@comma{} this includes provisioning tools such as Ansible@comma{} and orchestration tools such as Docker Compose and Kubernetes (which we will eventually get to). There are of course other choices too@comma{} but it is a rather neat idea to be able to use a single grammar for all types of configurations. It is claimed that YAML is more readable than JSON (which I doubt) and XML (which I agree with). Below is a quick rundown of relevant YAML syntax (there is more@comma{} but this covers 90% of the cloud configuration use cases).

@example
---                          # Every new YAML node starts with three hyphens. 
                             # You can often include many nodes in the same file.
Name: "Arthur Dent"          # Something with the key "Name" has the 
                             # (string) value "Arthur Dent"
Age: 42                      # Integers and floats are also supported.
Active: true                 # ... as are booleans (true or false)
Friends: null                # ... and null values.
Inventory:                   # The value of the inventory key is a list.
  - Tea
  - No Tea
  - Towel
  - Babelfish
Desires: [ House@comma{} Clothes ]   # Lists can also be inlined
Address:                      # A Mapping (or a dictionary) is a set of 
                              # key/value pairs@comma{} where each key is unique.
  Street: "Vogon Intergalactic Bypass #47111"
  City: "What do you mean city? We work with the whole Universe!"
  HouseNumber: 0
Contacts:                     # A list of mappings
  - Name: "Ford Prefect"
    Address: "Anywhere@comma{} really. Just wave!"
  - Name: "Trillian"
    Address: "Heart of Gold"
  - Name: "Marvin"
    Address: "Everywhere@comma{} eventually."
Appearances:                  # Inlined mappings
  - @{Title: "The Hitchhiker's Guide to the Galaxy"@comma{} Year: 1979@}
  - @{Title: "The Restaurant at the End of the Universe"@comma{} Year: 1980@}
  - @{Title: "Life@comma{} the Universe and Everything"@comma{} Year: 1982@}
  - @{Title: "So Long@comma{} and thanks for All the Fish"@comma{} Year: 1984@}
  - @{Title: "Mostly Harmless"@comma{} Year: 1992@}
FavouritePoem: |             # Multi-line contents.
  Oh freddled gruntbuggly@comma{}
  Thy micturations are to me@comma{} (with big yawning)
  As plurdled gabbleblotchits@comma{} in midsummer morning
  On a lurgid bee@comma{}
  That mordiously hath blurted out@comma{}
  Its earted jurtles@comma{} grumbling
  Into a rancid festering confectious organ squealer.
     [drowned out by moaning and screaming]
...     # Formally@comma{} a YAML ends with three dots. Can often be left out.
@end example

Do note that indentation matters. Apparently this is a popular design decision these days. I personally dislike this since I claim that whitespace should not carry semantic meaning. In my not so humble opinion@comma{} this choice decreases maintainability and readability.

On to Docker Compose@comma{} then@comma{} the configuration of which is specified in a yaml file (Please see @uref{https://docs.docker.com/compose/compose-file/} for a full reference). In summary@comma{} the following can be specified:

@itemize
@item
@samp{Services} (required) correspond to your different microservices@comma{} i.e. the components with which you will build your system@comma{} and for each service you specify its configuration@comma{} and how to scale it etc.
@item
@samp{Networks} Can be used to separate parts of your application into smaller networks. Usually@comma{} there is not much configuration to do per network.
@item
@samp{Volumes} define persistent storage volumes. Persistent is key here; if you are only using a temporary storage inside a container@comma{} you do not need a volume@comma{} but if you want to store data that survives restarts of the container (or scaling of it)@comma{} then you define a volume for this data. Typically@comma{} you will at least want volumes for use by your database service.
@item
@samp{Configs} are used to modify the configuration inside a container without having to rebuild the image. For example@comma{} this can be done by superimposing a new configuration file over the one in the image.
@item
@samp{Secrets} a special type of configs for sensitive data. It is good practice to keep configs and secrets separate to avoid accidentally exposing them (e.g. by committing a secret in the configuration management tool).
@end itemize

A brief example:

@example
services:
  name-of-some-service:
    image:            # docker image to base this service on
    ports:            # should match what you wrote in your Dockerfile
      - "host:container"
      - "8080:3000"
    volumes:
      - "volume:container-path"
      - db-data:/etc/data  # Mount the volume db-data inside 
                           # the container on the path /etc/data.
  some-other-service:
    image:           # the image of some other service you want to run

volumes:
  db-data:
@end example

@dots{} And at this level@comma{} there really isn't much more to say about the yaml syntax.

