@node Introducing Version 2 of the QuoteFinder app
@section Introducing Version 2 of the QuoteFinder app

@pindex QuoteFinder Version 2
Version 2 of the QuoteFinder app replaces the rudimentary search algorithm in the first version with something more complex that is able to look for the words @emph{near} each other and not just directly adjacent to each other@comma{} and instead of returning just a certain number of characters around where the words were found@comma{} this version returns the full sentences where the words were found.

I know. This is still not very complex and we could just as well continue to run this new search algorithm in a single service@comma{} but let us @emph{imagine} that this is super-complex and require a not insignificant amount of time to go through a single text. Let us imagine that as the number of books in our database grows@comma{} a single user request to look for a quote will require the help of several worker nodes sifting through the database.

The first and most obvious thing to do is to define an architecture that enables horizontal scaling@comma{} and that makes use of a message queue to dispatch jobs to the worker nodes.

@strong{QFApp Frontend}
The first microservice@comma{} QFApp@comma{} is similar to the previous QFStandalone@comma{} in that it is an express app. Much of the code is similar@comma{} including the use of a MongoDB database to store the texts in. The main difference lies in what happens in the @samp{searchTexts()} function. Rather than actually performing the search and format the resulting output@comma{} we are now invoking a @samp{Dispatcher} to distribute the search jobs onto a message queue to be consumed elsewhere by a number of @samp{QFWorkers}.

@pindex Redis
@pindex RSMQ
The message queue used by the Dispatcher is really the first good hit I found on NPM@comma{} called the Real Simple Message Queue@comma{} or @samp{RSMQ}. To be precise@comma{} I prefer the promise-enabled version of this queue @samp{RSMQPromise}. This queue makes use of a Redis key-value store to enqueue and dequeue messages to and from@comma{} so we need to remember to add this to our deployment architecture.

A challenge here@comma{} which should be a warning flag that this app is not really a good candidate for the queue centric architecture pattern@comma{} is that once the worker nodes are done they need a way to return what they find so that this can be returned to the web user. In this implementation the Dispatcher sets up a return queue@comma{} sends the jobs@comma{} waits for messages on the return queue@comma{} and then sends these on to the web interface using @samp{socket.io} emit-calls.

@strong{QFWorker Backend}
In contrast@comma{} the backend is much simpler. It connects to the job queue and listens for messages. The first one to pick a message gets it@comma{} and will go to town trying to find all the quotes. Once found@comma{} these are sent on the return queue@comma{} and eventually the QFWorker will either pick the next message if there is one@comma{} or go to sleep waiting for a new job.

To make things more interesting and to continue imagining that this is a complex and resource intensive application@comma{} I have added a bit of vertical scaling in here as well: Every job consists of a single title in the database@comma{} and this title is split up into chunks (of @samp{CHUNKSIZE=1000} lines)@comma{} and a number of chunks ( @samp{MAXTHREADS=10} ) are searched in parallel.

@strong{Data Format}
One reason for this decision is that I want you to get used to thinking about your data architecture as well as your application and deployment architectures.

Consider this: Each text is added to the database once@comma{} but is searched multiple times. If we keep the text as a single block in the database but still want to parallelise the search algorithm@comma{} then for every query we need to (a) fetch each entire text at once@comma{} and (b) split it into the desired chunk size. If we instead do this when we first add the text to the database@comma{} each search is made simpler because it only needs to fetch as many pre-split chunks as it currently need from the database@comma{} and then work on these. Less work for each search and less data to fetch before starting to analyse implies that we can start returning results sooner.

@strong{Summary}
A conceptual view of QuoteFinder Version 2:
@example
+--------------+       +----------------+--------------+            +---------------+
| Web Client   +-------+ QFApp(Express) | Dispatch     |            | QFWorker      |
+--------------+       +----------+-----+------^-------+            +-----^--+------+
                                  |            |                          |  |
                                  |            |        +-----------+     |  |
                                  |            |        | Message   |     |  |
                                  |            +--------> Queue     <-----+  |
                                  |                     +-----+-----+        |
                                  |                           |              |
                              +---+--------+            +-----+-----+        |
                              | MongoDB    |            | Redis     |        |
                              +---+--------+            +-----------+        |
                                  |                                          |
                                  +------------------------------------------+
@end example

