@node Installing and Running Version 2 of QuoteFinder
@section Installing and Running Version 2 of QuoteFinder

You have already installed the required software stack (@code{docker} and @code{docker-compose})@comma{} so this time we can get started directly with defining the deployment architecture.

@strong{Tasks}
@enumerate 0
@item
Open up a terminal and enter your @code{QuoteFinder} directory.

@item
Clone the QuoteFinder repository: @code{git clone https://github.com/mickesv/QuoteFinder/}  (If you did this in the previous practice round@comma{} git will throw an error but that's ok)  extract Version 2 by copying it @code{cp -r QuoteFinder/Containers/Version2 .}  @comma{} and cd into the directory @code{cd Version2}

@item
This time you will find two sub-directories here@comma{} one for @samp{QFApp} and one for @samp{QFWorker}. As before@comma{} inside these directories you will find a Dockerfile. Please take a moment to study these two files. You will notice that they are almost the same. In fact@comma{} the only differences are that QFApp will expose port 3000@comma{} and that the @samp{DEBUG} environment variable is different.

@item
Build the two images with the tag-names @samp{qfapp} and @samp{qfworker}@comma{} respectively.

@item
MongoDB is already downloaded@comma{} but you need to pull @samp{redis:alpine} as well.

@item
Back up to the directory @code{QuoteFinder/Version2/} and Create a file @samp{docker-compose-v2.yml}.
@end enumerate

In this file@comma{} you want to define the following @samp{services}:

@itemize
@item
@samp{app} is based on the @samp{qfapp} image
@itemize
@item
make sure @samp{ports} 8080:3000 are forwarded (port 3000 on the guest is forwarded to port 8080 on the host)
@item
the following @samp{environment} variables are set:
@samp{REDIS_HOST: messagequeue}
@samp{TEXTSTORE_HOST: textstore}
@end itemize
@item
@samp{worker} is based on the @samp{qfworker} image
@itemize
@item
the following @samp{environment} variables are set:
@samp{REDIS_HOST: messagequeue}
@samp{TEXTSTORE_HOST: textstore}
@end itemize
@item
@samp{messagequeue} is based on the image @samp{redis:alpine}
@itemize
@item
its starting @samp{command} is @samp{redis-server}
@item
it @samp{expose} the port ``6379''
@item
it mounts the following @samp{volumes}
@itemize
@item
@samp{redis-data:/data}
@item
@samp{redis-conf:/usr/local/etc/redis/redis.conf}
@end itemize
@end itemize
@item
@samp{textstore} is based on the image @samp{mongo}
@itemize
@item
its @samp{command} is @samp{--quiet --syslog}
@item
it @samp{expose} the port ``27017''
@item
it mounts the following @samp{volumes}
@itemize
@item
@samp{textstore-data:/data/db}
@item
@samp{mongo-config:/data/configdb}
@end itemize
@end itemize
@end itemize

The volumes used need to be defined (it is@comma{} however@comma{} enough to mention them@comma{} no further configuration is necessary):

@example
volumes:
  redis-data:
  redis-conf:
  textstore-data:
  mongo-config:
@end example

Note that you are intentionally left to explore the docker-compose syntax on your own here. Play around and give it a try before cheating. And no@comma{} I'm not going to tell you how to cheat.

@enumerate 7
@item
Run @code{docker compose -f docker-compose-v2.yml up}  to start your setup.

@item
Open a web browser to @uref{http://localhost:8080/add} and click the 'Add' button to add a book to your database. Your terminal window where the app is running will print some info@comma{} hopefully ending with @emph{Text added.} If not@comma{} make note of any error messages printed.

@item
Go to @uref{http://localhost:8080/} and search for something@comma{} e.g. 'prince'. This will give a list of hits. Remember to keep an eye on the output in the terminal.
@end enumerate

@node Editing the Running Application
@section Editing the Running Application

In case you happened to skip directly to the search step@comma{} you will notice that the search fails on a timeout and the worker node does not seem to be functioning at all. Let us see if we can recreate this bug. In the console@comma{} press @samp{Ctrl-C} to stop the running deployment@comma{} then re-run the same command as before but go directly to the search page @uref{http://localhost:8080/} and repeat the previous search. If you had already added something@comma{} it is still there and you can not reproduce the bug. Please take a moment to consider why this is the case.

@strong{Tasks} 
@enumerate
@item
Inspect the running deployment. Open another terminal and run @code{docker ps}. You should see four containers running. You might also wish to see what volumes are available: @code{docker volume ls}. Among other volumes you should see the four volumes defined in your yml-file. We can not remove them directly because they are in use by running containers (try! The command is @code{docker volume rm <volume-name>} ). Stopping the deployment doesn't help either@comma{} the volumes are apparently still in use. 

@item
Stop the deployment with @samp{Ctrl-C} and run @code{docker ps}. As you notice@comma{} the list is empty. You need to look for @emph{all}@comma{} even exited containers@comma{} with the command @code{docker ps -a} to see them. Let us remove all the containers running or not. I will do this in a lazy way with the command @code{docker rm -f $(docker ps -a -q) dummy}  (the nested command lists only the IDs of the containers@comma{} and the outer command removes them. 'dummy' is appended just to ensure that there is something -- anything -- there to look for so the outer command knows to behave nicely).

@item
It should now be possible to remove the volumes. Please do@comma{} and then restart the deployment again. Visit the search page @uref{http://localhost:8080/} @comma{} search for something@comma{} and see it timeout and fail.
@end enumerate

@cindex Docker Bind Mount
@enumerate
@item
Assume that we now want to edit the application@comma{} as we did before. With its current deployment@comma{} this will not work; each container is only based on the initial image (except redis and mongo that use the defined volumes). In order to enable editing we must -- as before -- mount a directory on the host computer to a directory inside the container (this is referred to as a @emph{bind mount} in docker vernacular). Edit the @code{docker-compose-v2.yml} file and add @code{volumes} to the services @code{app} ( @code{./QFApp/src:/app/src} ) and @code{worker} ( @code{./QFWorker/src:/app/src} ).

@item
Restart the deployment (technically@comma{} you only need to re-read the configuration@comma{} but for now it is easy enough to just @samp{Ctrl-C Up Enter} to restart).

@item
On your host machine@comma{} open up the file @code{QFApp/src/dispatcher.js} and familiarise yourself with the code. In the method @code{formatJobs()} we want to add the following at the start of the function:
@end enumerate

@example
if (0 == texts.length) @{
    console.log('WARNING: No texts are available@comma{} no jobs will be created.');
@}
@end example

As before@comma{} you should see the app reload directly (in the console output)@comma{} and if you search for something in the web browser before adding any texts this new warning will be printed in the console.

@node Scaling the Deployment
@section Scaling the Deployment

There is one last thing we wish to do with the running deployment: Scale it. With @code{docker compose} this is in fact quite easy:

@strong{Tasks}
@enumerate
@item
Edit the file @code{docker-compose-v2.yml} and add the following instruction to the @code{worker} service:
@end enumerate

@findex Docker Compose deploy
@findex Docker Compose replicas
@example
deploy:
  replicas: 3
@end example

@enumerate 2
@item
in a terminal where you are not running the @code{docker compose ... up} command@comma{} enter the directory where the file  @code{docker-compose-v2.yml} is located@comma{} and run the command @code{docker compose -f docker-compose-v2.yml up -d} (Note the 'detach' flag @code{-d}@comma{} this is what does all the magic in this case). Look at the output in the first terminal@comma{} you should see that there are now three replicas of the worker node running.

@item
Edit the number of replicas down to 1 again@comma{} re-run the @code{docker compose .. up -d} command and see what happens with the running deployment.
@end enumerate

@cindex Load Balancer
In theory@comma{} it is possible to scale the @code{app} service in the same way. In practice@comma{} however@comma{} this will not work. The reason for this is that the web client uses @code{socket.io} for communication@comma{} which essentially ties one web client to one running app container. The built-in load balancer in docker compose employs a simple round-robin algorithm@comma{} passing each new call to localhost:8080 to port 3000 in the next @code{app} container.

@node Cleanup and Summary
@section Cleanup and Summary

Stop and clean up after the running deployment by running the following commands:

@example
docker compose -f docker-compose-v2.yml stop
docker rm -f $(docker ps -a -q) dummy
docker volume rm version2_mongo-config version2_redis-conf\
       version2_redis-data version2_textstore-data
docker network prune -f
@end example

The last command may need a clarification: Even if we have not manually created any virtual networks@comma{} docker compose have still created them in the background. @code{docker network ls} will show you any remaining virtual networks.

@strong{Summary}
We have now:
@enumerate
@item
Built two Docker images@comma{} corresponding to the frontend @code{qfapp} and the backend @code{qfworker} of the QuoteFinder version 2 app.
@item
Downloaded a @samp{redis:alpine} image.
@item
Constructed a docker-compose configuration that defines the four services and the volumes needed for this version to run.
@item
Launched this deployment
@item
Modified the configuration to use @emph{build mounts} to superimpose the host's version of the application code over the default code inside the images.
@item
Edited a file locally and see the app in the running deployment restart to reflect the updated version.
@item
Modified the configuration to add more @emph{replicas} of the @code{worker} service.
@item
Re-deployed the configuration to the running deployment@comma{} without a restart.
@end enumerate

Please take a moment to compare these achievements with the manual deployment of QuoteFinder Version 1.

@strong{Remaining Pain Points}
Using RSMQ for passing messages between the frontend and the backend turned out to be a bad idea for a number of reasons:

@enumerate
@item
The implementation of RMSQWorker is in fact @emph{polling} the Redis store@comma{} so there is an inevitable delay before jobs are picked up.
@item
That we create a unique return queue and pass that along in the job is a dependency injection@comma{} meaning that the containers are no longer independent of each other.
@item
The workers need to pass their output back to a waiting web client. This means that the queue centric workflow is not the most suitable design pattern.
@end enumerate

A more apt design pattern is to use a REST-API to access the QFWorkers; this is explored in Version 3 of the QuoteFinder app.

Application design aside@comma{} the use of docker compose poses a bigger problem: Docker Compose deploys locally and not to a cloud provider. Moreover@comma{} there is no easy mechanism to distribute containers across several hosts.

We can scale the QFWorker by adding more replicas@comma{} but we cannot easily scale the frontend QFApp. Making the frontend simpler@comma{} acting as a gateway to the QFWorkers who do the heavy lifting@comma{} is one approach to deal with this.

