@node Introducing Version 3 of the QuoteFinder app
@section Introducing Version 3 of the QuoteFinder app

A few issues continue to itch with QuoteFinder Version 2. Specifically@comma{} the use of a queue centric workflow for an application where this is not the most suitable choice@comma{} and the resulting bad performance due to the chosen queue implementation. Even more concerning is that the wrong choice of architecture pattern broke one of the fundamental principles of microservice design: @emph{Services should be independent}! Before continuing@comma{} let us take a moment to correct this.

In @uref{https://github.com/mickesv/QuoteFinder/} @comma{} that you have previously cloned@comma{} there is also a Version 3 of the QuoteFinder app. Please copy this version to a separate directory:

@example
cp -r QuoteFinder/Containers/Version3 .
cp QuoteFinder/docker-compose-v3.yml Version3/
cd Version3
ls
@end example

As before@comma{} you should see two directories@comma{} one each for @code{QFApp} and one for @code{QFWorker}. You also copied a file @code{docker-compose-v3.yml}@comma{} so let's start by inspecting this file:

@example
version: "3.8"
services:
  app:
    image: qfappv3
    ports:
      - 8080:3000
    volumes:
      - ./Containers/Version3/QFApp/src:/app/src
    environment:
      TEXTSTORE_HOST: textstore
      WORKER: worker
      TIMEOUT: 10

  worker:
    image: qfworkerv3
    volumes:
      - ./Containers/Version3/QFWorker/src:/app/src
    deploy:
      replicas: 5
    environment:
      TEXTSTORE_HOST: textstore

  textstore:
    image: mongo
    restart: always
    command: --quiet --syslog
    expose:
      - "27017"
    volumes:
      - textstore-data:/data/db
      - mongo-config:/data/configdb

volumes:
  textstore-data:
  mongo-config:
@end example

There are only three services this time since the Redis store is no longer needed. The images are tagged @code{v3} to keep them separate from the previous version (They could probably have been named @code{qfapp:v3} and @code{qfworker:v3} according to Docker's tag-numbering scheme@comma{} but they are not really versions of the same app since they use a different architecture@comma{} and I like to live on the wild side). The @code{app} service has one additional environment variable set@comma{} but otherwise there are no major differences.

@pindex diff
Inspecting the QFAapp frontend@comma{} the only part we expect to differ is the @code{Dispatch} class (This can be confirmed with the help of the command @code{diff}@comma{} for example using @code{diff --brief QFApp/src/ ../Version2/QFApp/src/} to get a summary of which files that differ). In the same way@comma{} we find that the only file that differs for the QFWorker is the @code{index.js} file.

The @code{Dispatch} class is now much simpler@comma{} since there is no need to meddle with any queues in any direction. Since it is now so small@comma{} let us inspect the class in its entirety:

@example
class Dispatcher @{
    constructor() @{
        this.TIMEOUT = 1000 * (process.env.TIMEOUT || DEFAULTTIMEOUT);
        this.WORKER = process.env.WORKER || DEFAULTWORKER;
    @}

    formatJobs(searchString@comma{} texts) @{
        return texts.map( t => @{ return @{searchString: searchString@comma{} textTitle: t@};@});
    @}

    dispatchSearch(searchString@comma{} jobs@comma{} socket) @{
        console.log('Searching for : ' + searchString);
        let baseurl = 'http://' + this.WORKER + ':3000';

        if (0 >= jobs.length) @{
            socket.emit('done'@comma{} @{msg: 'no texts available' @}); 
            return 'DONE'
        @};        

        setTimeout( () => socket.emit('done'@comma{} @{msg: 'timeout'@})@comma{} this.TIMEOUT);

        return Promise.all(jobs.map( j => @{
            let title = j.textTitle.replaceAll(' '@comma{}'+');
            let search = j.searchString.replaceAll(' '@comma{}'+');
            let url = baseurl + '/' + title + '/' +search;
            console.log('Using url:'@comma{} url);
            return fetch(url)
                .then(res => res.json())
                .then(res => 
                    res.forEach( t =>
                        socket.emit('answer'@comma{} JSON.stringify(t))));
        @}))
        .then( () => socket.emit('done'@comma{} @{msg: 'done'@}));
    @}
@}
@end example

As before@comma{} @code{formatJobs()} generates one job for each text in the database (and could be improved with the same modification we did for Version 2). The new magic start with the line @code{return Promise.all(jobs.map( j => @{} @comma{} where each job is used to generate a Promise to dispatch the search of that specific title to one worker. Each title will generate an asynchronous call to the REST API in a QFWorker (the load balancer in Docker Compose decides which one)@comma{} and when an answer is received it will use @code{socket.emit()} to send this answer to the web client.

The @code{index.js} in QFWorker is now slightly longer since it is now using the same @samp{express.js} framework as QFApp to serve up a tiny web server. However@comma{} most of the new code is boilerplate code for @samp{express.js}@comma{} and there is really only one method which takes care of the search requests. Please take a moment to study @code{index.js} and make sure you understand how the REST API is constructed.

Feel free to build the necessary images for Version 3@comma{} start the deployment and play around with it. It should be prepared for you so that you can live-edit the files and the deployment as you did with Version 2.

