@end menu

@node Introduction to Kubernetes
@section Introduction to Kubernetes

With the introduction of Docker Compose@comma{} it is now possible to provision and orchestrate a whole collection of docker microservices. The provisioning is done in a first step@comma{} where an initial docker image is created. Up to a point@comma{} the software in this image is ready to start running@comma{} requiring only a small set of additional run-time configuration. Docker compose is responsible for the next step@comma{} which is to orchestrate and deploy the specified services. However@comma{} Docker Compose only deploys locally.

@pindex Kubernetes
To deploy to a cloud provider@comma{} it is time to introduce the next tool in the tool chain@comma{} i.e. Kubernetes @uref{https://kubernetes.io/} . Somewhat simplified@comma{} one can say that Kubernetes is used to set up the deployment infrastructure within which the application microservices are run.

@strong{Some Kubernetes Terminology}
From the inside out:

@cindex Kubernetes Container
@cindex Kubernetes Pod
@cindex Kubernetes Deployment
@cindex Kubernetes Service
@cindex Kubernetes Volume
@cindex Kubernetes Label
@cindex Kubernetes Persistent Storage
@cindex Kubernetes Persistent Volume
@cindex Kubernetes Persistent Volume Claim

@enumerate
@item
@emph{Containers} are still the core concept@comma{} and they are no different to the microservice containers used previously. They are@comma{} in fact@comma{} based on the same docker images as before.
@item
Containers are collected into @emph{pods}@comma{} that gathers containers working closely together@comma{} possibly with some shared storage.
@item
A container can mount @emph{Volumes} inside the same pod.
@item
If the container need @emph{Persistent Storage} this is split into two parts. The developer of container makes a @emph{PersistentVolumeClaim} describing what type of persistent storage they need@comma{} and the administrator of the Kubernetes cluster provides one or more @emph{PersistentVolume} to match the desired claims.
@item
A @emph{Deployment} describes the contents in a Pod on a slightly higher level@comma{} enabling Kubernetes to take more responsibility for e.g. scaling the deployment or to move the pods to other nodes if necessary.
@item
@emph{Labels} are used to identify which parts of the application are affected by different configuration directives.
@item
@emph{Services} are set up to access your application. They act as the glue between the pods/containers and the outside world.
@end enumerate

The best way to come to terms with this is to walk through a practical example@comma{} which is what we'll do next.

@node Get Started with Kubernetes
@section Get Started with Kubernetes

@pindex kubectl
You have already installed the necessary software to set up a very small Kubernetes cluster (consisting of a single machine). For Windows/Mac@comma{} this is included in @code{Docker Desktop} @comma{} although you may need to go into the settings and enable Kubernetes. For Linux@comma{} you will use the standalone @code{minikube}@comma{} started with a simple @code{minikube start} . Check the status of your cluster with @code{kubectl cluster-info} .

It is possible to set up your deployment well and good using only the command line tool @code{kubectl} but I would not recommend it. By now@comma{} the mantra @emph{Infrastructure as Code} should be ingrained into you@comma{} so let's write some YAML code to set up a deployment.

@example
# QFStandalone
# --------------------
# - Deployment to launch one container of mickesv/qfstandalone in a pod.
# - Service (type: LoadBalancer) to open up the app to the world (localhost@comma{} at least).
# 
---
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: qfapp-service
  labels:
    app: qfapp
spec:
  type: LoadBalancer
  ports:
  - name: http
    port: 3000
    nodePort: 30001
    protocol: TCP
  selector:
    app: qfapp
---
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: qfstandalone
  labels:
    app: qfapp
spec:
  replicas: 1
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: qfapp
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: qfapp
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: qfstandalone
        image: mickesv/qfstandalone:version1
        ports:
        - containerPort: 3000
---
@end example

@cindex Kubernetes ReplicaSet
A quick summary of what can be seen:
@itemize
@item
This consist of two yaml blocks (separated by three hyphens @samp{---} )@comma{} where the first define a deployment and the second exposes this deployment through the use of a LoadBalancer Service.
@item
We still have the same issue as before with the use of socket.io and the load balancer@comma{} so for now we keep the number of replicas to 1. Giving this instructions means that Kubernetes will create a @samp{ReplicaSet} to keep track of the pods in this deployment.
@item
The Deployment named @samp{qfstandalone} has a one template for a pod@comma{} with the label @samp{qfapp}. As it happens@comma{} the selector instructs the ReplicaSet to manage pods matching this same label.
@item
The pod template says that there should be one container in there@comma{} with the name @samp{qfstandalone} and based on the image @samp{mickesv/qfstandalone:version1}.
@item
Notice that the @samp{containers} is a list@comma{} so it is possible to add other containers into the same pod.
@item
The Service is created to act as a LoadBalancer against the ReplicaSet Deployment. Internally@comma{} it will access port 3000@comma{} but outside of the cluster (or from other pods inside the cluster)@comma{} this will be accessed via port 30001.
@end itemize

@strong{Cave!} Kubernetes will fetch the images from Docker hub@comma{} so you either need to push your own qfstandalone image thither or you can trust me (!) and use the @samp{mickesv} version.

@strong{Tasks}
@enumerate 0
@item
Open up a terminal and enter your @code{QuoteFinder} directory. Create a new sub-directory@comma{} e.g. @code{QuoteFinder/Kubernetes} @comma{} and cd into it.

@item
Create the file @code{qfstandalone-v1-1.yaml} and copy the configuration from above into it.

@item
Apply the configuration: @code{kubectl apply -f qfstandalone-v1-1.yaml}

@item
Check what was created: @code{kubectl get all}
@end enumerate

@tindex Kubernetes Cluster IP
@tindex Kubernetes External IP
@enumerate
@item
It is time to test if the web page is available. In order to access the web page@comma{} we first need to find the external ip of the qfapp-service (Remember that the service is required to bridge from the outside world to inside the Kubernetes cluster). Get the list of services through @code{kubectl get services} @comma{} and you will notice that each service has both a @samp{CLUSTER-IP} and an @samp{EXTERNAL-IP}. Right now@comma{} the service only has a Cluster-ip@comma{} which is internal to Kubernetes. Other containers inside the same Kubernetes cluster may use this to access the qfapp-service@comma{} but we cannot use this address from the outside. For this@comma{} we need the external IP@comma{} and this may still be listed as @samp{<pending>}.
@end enumerate

@cindex Kubernetes NodePort
On Windows or Mac with Docker Desktop you may have better luck. Since the LoadBalancer service was created with just a @code{nodePort} specified@comma{} Kubernetes will open this port on the node (Right now@comma{} you only have one node; your host computer). Thus@comma{} the QuoteFinder app may be available as @uref{http://localhost:30001/} . 

On Linux with Minikube@comma{} the service is exposed with @code{minikube service qfapp-service} . As a courtesy to you@comma{} minikube will open this web page for you.

@enumerate 5
@item
Previously we were able to see the containers print out all sorts of debug info as it was running. Let's try recreating that. First@comma{} list all running pods (just the names): @code{kubectl get pods} . Notice that the name you specified has been appended with some sort of hash key. All of this is the name of the pod.
@end enumerate

The command to access the logs in a pod is @code{kubectl logs -f <podname>}@comma{} where the @code{-f} flag means that we want to @emph{follow} the logs and continue to print anything that is added to them. With a bit of Unix elbow-grease@comma{} the following incantation should both find the name of the pod and attach itself to the log: @code{kubectl get pods -o name | grep qfstandalone | xargs kubectl logs -f} . 

@pindex grep
@pindex xargs
@pindex Unix pipe |
Breaking down the command@comma{} we see the following:
@itemize
@item
The three parts are combined with Unix pipes (the @code{|} symbol). The output (standard output@comma{} to be precise) of the command to the left of the pipe will be the input (standard input) for the command to the right of the pipe. This is how simple Unix commands are daisy-chained to each other to produce ever more complex tasks.
@item
@code{kubectl get pods -o name} prints the names (only the names) of all running pods.
@item
@code{grep qfstandalone} filters the output to only those containing the string ``qfstandalone''
@item
@code{xargs} converts whatever it receives on standard input into arguments at the end of whatever command is given to it.
@item
@code{kubectl logs -f} is the start of this command@comma{} to which xargs add the names of all pods containing the string ``qfstandalone'' in their names.
@end itemize

The end result is that we are now attached to the logs and can see the same type of output in the console as we were able to do previously. Incidentally@comma{} you may notice that users seem to connect from some strange IP address; while this @emph{should} be the address of your host computer@comma{} it clearly is not. Take a moment to consider the deployment and see if you can't figure out whose IP address this is.

@enumerate 6
@item
While you are attached to the logs try searching for something on the web page. This will not work since we have not yet connected our database or told the qfapp container where to find it@comma{} but the log output is interesting. (you will@comma{} for example@comma{} get a warning that @samp{TEXTSTORE_HOST} is not set).
@end enumerate


@strong{Summary}
At this point we have:
@enumerate
@item
Started a local Kubernetes Cluster consisting of a single Node.
@item
Defined a Kubernetes ReplicaSet Deployment where containers matching the label @samp{app: qfapp} is deployed.
@item
Re-used the same QuoteFinder v1 (qfstandalone) docker image as before and deployed it on this cluster.
@item
Started a Kubernetes LoadBalancer Service to enable access from outside the Kubernetes cluster to the ReplicaSet deployment.
@end enumerate


@strong{Cleanup}
Clean up your deployment: @code{kubectl delete --cascade='foreground' -f qfstandalone-v1-1.yaml} .

@node Attach the Database
@section Attach the Database

Attaching a database should in theory be easy@comma{} but let's take a moment and do this ``right''@comma{} so that we get a configurable and by the books deployment.

@cindex Kubernetes StatefulSet
First@comma{} of course@comma{} the database need to be started. Since we want data to survive restarts@comma{} we will do this as a @code{StatefulSet} . This StatefulSet will launch an image of MongoDB (as before). It will also make two persistent VolumeClaims (as before); one for MongoDB's configuration and one for the data.

@cindex Kubernetes Access Roles
These Persistent Volume Claims are then matched to Persistent Volumes by Kubernetes@comma{} and the recommendation is that these are specified in a separate file@comma{} so that they can be deployed by someone with the right access privileges. App developers make claims@comma{} and node administrators provide resources. A Persistent Volume is a resource@comma{} and thus need a node administrator to configure.

For now@comma{} however@comma{} there is no need to specify these Persistent Volumes; at least @code{minikube} will create them as needed. Other tools may not@comma{} and once you deploy to a cloud provider@comma{} you most certainly will need to define the Persistent Volumes somewhere and somehow. For now@comma{} however@comma{} we can leave this.

@cindex Kubernetes Persistent VolumeClaim Templates
What we @emph{do} need to take care of@comma{} however@comma{} is a little snag in how Persistent Volume Claims work. Let's say we decide that we want more replicas of our database container in the StatefulSet. Unless otherwise specified@comma{} Kubernetes will map all of these replicas @emph{to the same} volume claim@comma{} and MongoDB will most certainly not like this. The first replica will lock the database files@comma{} and no other replicas will be allowed to access them.

@tindex Kubernetes volumeClaimTemplates
To avoid this@comma{} we will not make concrete claims to a particular persistent volume; we will specify a @emph{template} for the claims@comma{} called a @code{volumeClaimTemplate}. This template specifies the @emph{type} of volume required@comma{} but as little else as possible. Especially avoid specifying any storageClassName@comma{} or any StorageClasses or PersistentVolumes at all.

Almost finally@comma{} in order to access the replicas of the database@comma{} we need a Service@comma{} and any access (even from within the Kubernetes cluster) will be done with the help of this service.

@cindex Kubernetes ConfigMap
We still need to configure the QuoteFinder app so that it can find and connect to the database. One Kubernetes way to do this is via a @code{ConfigMap}. This is a simple key-value map that can be referenced from the container templates.

Enough talking! Time to get busy.

@strong{Tasks}
@enumerate 0
@item
Copy the previous yaml file to a new file@comma{} to keep a record of the different steps: @code{cp qfstandalone-v1-1.yaml qfstandalone-v1-2.yaml}

@item
Add the following to the end of this new copy:
@end enumerate

@example
# TextStore
# --------------------
# - one container per pod running the image mongodb
# - one headless service to access them.
# - Two persistentVolumeClaims/mounts: textstore-data@comma{} and mongo-config
---
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: textstore-service
  labels:
    app: textstore
spec:
  ports:
  - port: 27017
    targetPort: mongodb-port
  selector:
    app: textstore
---
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: StatefulSet
metadata:
  name: textstore
  labels:
    app: textstore
spec:
  serviceName: mongodb
  replicas: 1
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: textstore
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: textstore
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: textstore
        image: mongo
        ports:
        - containerPort: 27017
          name: mongodb-port
        volumeMounts:
        - name: textstore-data
          mountPath: /data/db
        - name: mongo-config
          mountPath: /data/configdb
  volumeClaimTemplates:
    - metadata:
        name: textstore-data
      spec:
        accessModes: ["ReadWriteOnce"]
        resources:
          requests: 
            storage: 10Mi
    - metadata:
        name: mongo-config
      spec:
        accessModes: ["ReadWriteOnce"]
        resources:
          requests: 
            storage: 5Mi          
---
@end example

Especially interesting here is the @code{volumeClaimTemplates} . As you notice@comma{} the claims are made in terms of how we are going to access them (``ReadWriteOnce'')@comma{} and their desired size. The @code{metadata.name} match the @code{volumeMounts} in the container spec above.

@enumerate 2
@item
Also append the following:
@end enumerate

@example
# ConfigMap to store TEXTSTORE_HOST variable in
# --------------------
---
apiVersion: v1
kind: ConfigMap
metadata:
  name: qfapp-config
  labels:
    data: config
data:
  textstore_host: "NOTSET"
---
@end example

Caution@comma{} @code{textstore_host} MUST be ``NOTSET'' (case sensitive). This will be replaced with the right IP during startup.

@enumerate 3
@item
Some edits are required for the qfapp container template to make use of this ConfigMap to set an environment variable (using the  @code{env} specification):
@end enumerate

@example
spec:
  containers:
  - name: qfstandalone
    image: mickesv/qfstandalone:version1
    ports:
    - containerPort: 3000
    env:
    - name: TEXTSTORE_HOST
      valueFrom:
        configMapKeyRef: 
          name: qfapp-config
          key: textstore_host
@end example

@enumerate 4
@item
Time to deploy all of this. This needs to be done in stages. First@comma{} the ConfigMap and the textstore database are created. Then@comma{} the Cluster-IP address for the database is extracted so that the ConfigMap can be updated accordingly. Finally@comma{} the qfapp deployment can be launched:
@end enumerate

@example
# Apply only the parts matching the label for the configmap and the textStore
kubectl apply -f qfstandalone-v1-2.yaml -l data=config
kubectl apply -f qfstandalone-v1-2.yaml -l app=textstore

# Extract the clusterIP
export textstoreip=$( kubectl get \
                              services/textstore-service \
                              --template='@{@{.spec.clusterIP@}@}' )

# Retrieve the ConfigMap@comma{} replace "NOTSET" with the clusterIP@comma{} and re-apply.
kubectl get configmap/qfapp-config -o yaml\
    | sed -r "s/NOTSET/$textstoreip/" | kubectl apply -f -

# Finally@comma{} start the qfapp
kubectl apply -f qfstandalone-v1-2.yaml -l app=qfapp
@end example

@dots{}  If I were you I would seriously considering making a script for this@comma{} since it is only going to get worse.

@enumerate 5
@item
Attach a log to the running instance of qfstandalone: @code{kubectl get pods -o name | grep qfstandalone | head -1 | xargs kubectl logs -f}

@item
Using a separate terminal@comma{} open a web browser to your service: @code{minikube service qfapp-service} (or equivalent command for your version of Kubernetes)  and search for something. Note in the log that the connection to the database is established and the search is executed as it should. Do try to add some texts and re-do the search if you like.
@end enumerate

@strong{Summary}
We have now:
@enumerate
@item
Added a database container and a service to access it.
@item
Added a ConfigMap where we can keep the internal IP-address to this service.
@item
Updated the qfstandalone deployment to make use of this ConfigMap.
@item
Deployed in three stages; start ConfigMap and textstore@comma{} extract the Cluster-IP for the textStore and update the ConfigMap@comma{} and started the qfstandalone app and its corresponding service.
@end enumerate

@strong{Cleanup}
As before@comma{} clean up your deployment: @code{kubectl delete --cascade='foreground' -f qfstandalone-v1-2.yaml} .

