@end menu

@node Database Scaling
@section Database Scaling

@example
 ____________________________
< Can we scale the database? >
 ----------------------------
        \   ^__^
         \  (oo)\_______
            (__)\       )\/\
                ||----w |
                ||     ||
@end example

Yes. Yes@comma{} we can. The first and easiest way to scale is by scaling the node it runs on@comma{} i.e. @emph{vertical scaling}. This implies increasing the number of CPUs@comma{} the amount of RAM@comma{} and/or increase the storage capacity.

@cindex Database Replication
@cindex NoSQL
@cindex Eventual Consistency
The second way to scale is by @emph{replication}. This creates duplicate copies of the entire database@comma{} each replica requiring as much resources as a single instance of it. Typically@comma{} this is combined with a load balancer to even out the load between the replicas. This is typically good if the application @emph{reads} data more often than it @emph{writes}. Writing data will be slower since the edit will need to propagate across all replicas before it becomes available@comma{} but reading can be done by any single replica. In contrast to the classic relational databases@comma{} most NoSQL databases (such as MongoDB)@comma{} only promise @emph{eventual consistency}@comma{} and this is put to good use for read-intensive applications with database replication.

@cindex Database Sharding
@cindex Sharding Architectures
The @emph{third} way of scaling is to split the database across multiple nodes. This@comma{} in turn@comma{} can be done in some different ways. First@comma{} one may divvy up the tables across the available nodes. For simple queries it may be sufficient to just hit a single node@comma{} but for more complex joins it may be necessary to perform several queries to get data from tables distributed to other nodes as well. Simplifying a little (ok; a lot) one may see this as @emph{splitting the database across columns}.

When@comma{} instead@comma{} the database is split across rows so that some @emph{rows} are available on one node and other rows are available on another node@comma{} this is called @emph{Sharding}. The simplest way to do this is to specify ranges for one key and assign a shard ID for each range. Stepping up@comma{} one may have an arbitrarily complex algorithm to compute the shard ID for each database record.

Abandoning all hopes of being able to apply Boyce-Codd Normal Form@comma{} it is of course desirable to keep related data together on the same node. Rather than splitting data across tables and use joins when querying@comma{} each record can be designed to contain all the necessary data. These records can then be distributed based on some geographical key@comma{} for example to comply with laws governing where citizens' data must be located.

@cindex Madness
Of course@comma{} one can split across columns as well through sharding. One may even use different sharding architectures for each table. Madness only ever lies a single design decision away.

@node MongoDB ReplicaSet
@section MongoDB ReplicaSet

For the sake of selling access to various tools@comma{} everyone agrees that Database Sharding is complex and painful. So in this course we will focus on the next best step@comma{} which is to set up a @emph{replicated} database. We will get some help from Kubernetes@comma{} but since each NoSQL database implements this in their very own creative way this is also going to involve a certain amount of tool specific (MongoDB) incantation.

@cindex MongoDB ReplicaSet
Conceptually@comma{} replication ought to work as illustrated below:

@example
 _O_          +----------------------+        +--------------------+
  |     ------+ Service/LoadBalancer \--------+ MongoDB Replica    |
 / \          +----------------------+\\-     +--------------------+
Client                                 \ \-
                                        \  \- +--------------------+
                                         \-  \+ MongoDB Replica    |
                                           \  +--------------------+
                                            \
                                             \+--------------------+
                                              \ MongoDB Replica    |
                                              +--------------------+
@end example

In practice@comma{} however@comma{} it doesn't. MongoDB does not know that there is such a thing as external Load-Balancers (and in any case it can not rely on that the user knows). The MongoDB replica do also not want to assume this role@comma{} and if they did they would still need to decide which IP should be broadcast to the outside. Instead@comma{} the design solution that MongoDB has opted for is to push load balancing onto the database clients.

By now you know that I have plenty of opinions about software and design decisions so I will leave it as an exercise to the reader to define@comma{} list@comma{} and sort a suitable array of invectives.

@node Setting up a MongoDB ReplicaSet in Kubernetes
@section Setting up a MongoDB ReplicaSet in Kubernetes

The following items from the previous setup are generic enough that they do not require any modification at this stage:

@table @asis
@item The qfapp-service
this service only enables access from the outside of the Kubernetes cluster@comma{} so there is no need to change it.
@item The textstore-service
this too will remain unchanged. To be honest@comma{} I'm not sure whether it is actually used@comma{} but it may need to be there anyway.
@item The volumeClaimTemplates for the textstore containers
These are the same as before and will remain unchanged.
@end table

@enumerate
@item
@anchor{TextStore StatefulSet}TextStore StatefulSet


The @samp{textstore StatefulSet}@comma{} does require some changes:

@cindex Kubernetes Readiness Probe
@itemize
@item
For cosmetic reasons@comma{} we replace the @samp{serviceName} to ``textstore-service''. This becomes part of the hostname (e.g. @code{textstore-0.textstore-service.default.svc.cluster.local})
@item
The number of @samp{replicas} starts out with the value @samp{3}.
@item
The startup @samp{command} needs to be edited to start MongoDB in ReplicaSet mode: @samp{["mongod"@comma{} "--bind_ip"@comma{} "0.0.0.0"@comma{} "--replSet"@comma{} "MainRepSet"} . Breaking this down:
@itemize
@item
listen to all network interfaces @samp{--bind_ip 0.0.0.0}
@item
use the specified name for the ReplicaSet @samp{--replSet MainRepSet}
@end itemize
@item
A @emph{Readiness Probe} is defined. This will be used by Kubernetes to check whether the startup of a replica is ready and it can be added to the list of running replicas. Running a ``ping'' command inside the database is usually a good start for this.
@end itemize

In summary@comma{} the following yaml defines the textstore StatefulSet:

@example
---
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: StatefulSet
metadata:
  name: textstore
  labels:
    app: textstore
spec:
  serviceName: textstore-service
  replicas: 3
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: textstore
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: textstore
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: textstore
        image: mongo
        command: ["mongod"@comma{} "--bind_ip"@comma{} "0.0.0.0"@comma{} "--replSet"@comma{} "MainRepSet"]
        readinessProbe:
          exec:
            command: ["mongosh"@comma{} "--eval"@comma{} "db.adminCommand(@{ping: 1@})"]
        ports:
        - containerPort: 27017
          name: mongodb-port
        volumeMounts:
        - name: textstore-data
          mountPath: /data/db
        - name: mongo-config
          mountPath: /data/configdb
  volumeClaimTemplates:
    - metadata:
        name: textstore-data
      spec:
        accessModes: ["ReadWriteOnce"]
        resources:
          requests: 
            storage: 10Mi
    - metadata:
        name: mongo-config
      spec:
        accessModes: ["ReadWriteOnce"]
        resources:
          requests: 
            storage: 5Mi
---
@end example

@item
@anchor{Qfapp ConfigMap}Qfapp ConfigMap


Accessing the database@comma{} will require a new access string:

@example
  ---
  apiVersion: v1
  kind: ConfigMap
  metadata:
    name: qfapp-config
    labels:
      data: config
  data:
    textstore_host: "NOTSET"
    textstore_replicaset: "mongodb://textstore-0.textstore-service.default.svc.cluster.local:27017@comma{}\
textstore-1.textstore-service.default.svc.cluster.local:27017@comma{}\
textstore-2.textstore-service.default.svc.cluster.local:27017/\
textStore?replicaSet=MainRepSet"
  ---
@end example

I am not a fan of that the URLs to each member in the cluster has to be hard-coded like this ( @samp{textstore_replicaset} )@comma{} since this limits the ability to scale up or down the number of replicas. Ideally@comma{} it should be as easy to change the @samp{spec.replicas} for the StatefulSet and let Kubernetes re-apply. In practice@comma{} this is now made much more difficult.

@item
@anchor{QFStandalone Deployment}QFStandalone Deployment


As a start@comma{} the only edit to the qfstandalone deployment we make is to include the newly defined element from the ConfigMap. Specifically@comma{} the environment variable @samp{TEXTSTORE_HOST} need to get its value from @samp{textstore_replicaset} in the configmap.

@example
---
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: qfstandalone
  labels:
    app: qfapp
spec:
  replicas: 1
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: qfapp
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: qfapp
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: qfstandalone
        image: mickesv/qfstandalone:version1
        ports:
        - containerPort: 3000
        env:
        - name: TEXTSTORE_HOST
          valueFrom:
            configMapKeyRef: 
              name: qfapp-config
              key: textstore_replicaset
---
@end example

Do note that this will not be quite sufficient to create a workable solution. Can you already now figure out why this will not be sufficient?

@item
@anchor{Tasks}Tasks


@enumerate 0
@item
Copy the previous yaml file to a new file@comma{} to keep a record of the different steps: @code{cp qfstandalone-v1-2.yaml qfstandalone-v1-3.yaml}

@item
Locate the @samp{textstore StatefulSet} and replace it with the version above.

@item
Locate the @samp{QFApp ConfigMap} and replace it with the version above.

@item
Locate the @samp{QFStandalone Deployment} and replace it with the version above.

@item
Start everything: @code{kubectl apply -f qfstandalone-v1-3.yaml}

@item
Wait for the replicas to come online: @code{kubectl rollout status statefulset textstore --timeout=30s}
This may take longer than 30 seconds@comma{} so you may need to run the command a couple of times. If it @emph{does} take longer@comma{} it is an excellent opportunity to open up Kubernetes' dashboard and see what is going on: @samp{minikube dashboard &} 

@item
Once all three replicas are running (but not before!)@comma{} it is time to initiate the replicaset in MongoDB@. Run the following: @code{kubectl exec -it textstore-0 -- mongosh --quiet --eval "rs.initiate()"}
This will log in to @emph{one} of the members in the replicaset and initiate it. Sometimes@comma{} MongoDB has already figured this out@comma{} and you will receive a message ``already initialized''. This is ok.

@item
Double check the status: @code{kubectl exec -it textstore-0 -- mongosh --quiet --eval "rs.status()"}
One particular thing to look out for is to make sure that one of the servers has the @code{stateStr: 'PRIMARY'} . You may have to wait a while and check again for this.

@item
Connect to the logs in qfstandalone (as before): @code{kubectl get pods -o name | grep qfstandalone | head -1 | xargs kubectl logs -f}

@item
Connect to the web interface (e.g. with @code{minikube service qfapp-service} ) and search for something. Keep an eye on the logs in case it fails.
@end enumerate

Things ought to work at this stage; if not@comma{} the correct way is to go back to developing the app in docker. Strongly discouraged as it is@comma{} it is however still possible to enter through a back door to make small edits (don't even bother trying to do some fancy development in this way):

@enumerate 10
@item
Find the name of the qfstandalone pod: @code{kubectl get pods -o name | grep qfstandalone}

@item
Enter into this pod: @code{kubectl exec -it <podname> -- sh} (where ``podname'' is the name of the pod as found in the previous step.
Feel free to poke around at your own leisure here and see what you can find and what works. Note that in order to keep the container image small we used a very limited linux distribution as base@comma{} so there isn't much installed at all.

@item
Edit the file @code{/app/src/simpleTextManager.js} (I'm afraid the only editor you've got is @samp{vi}) . Make some simple edit and save. 
You should see the app restarting in the log output you attached to earlier.
@end enumerate

@strong{Summary}
We have now:
@enumerate
@item
Configured the simplest and unsafest possible MongoDB replicaset without any form of authentication whatsoever.
@item
Created a single-phase startup where everything can be deployed with a single @samp{kubectl apply} command.
@item
Waited for parts of a deployment to come online in an orderly fashion with @code{kubectl rollout status} and a ReadinessProbe.
@item
Ugly-hacked our way into a container to edit the running application code.
@end enumerate

@strong{Cleanup}
As before@comma{} clean up your deployment: @code{kubectl delete --cascade='foreground' -f qfstandalone-v1-3.yaml} .
@end enumerate

@node Edit a Container in a Pod in a Node in a Cluster
@chapter Edit a Container in a Pod in a Node in a Cluster

@anchor{kube-edit}

@example
 _______________________________________
( So why were we not able to use a Bind )
( Mount to access the innards of the    )
( container as before?                  )
 ---------------------------------------
        o   ^__^
         o  (oo)\_______
            (__)\       )\/\
                ||----w |
                ||     ||
@end example

@cindex Bind Mount
Technically@comma{} we @emph{are} able to do this. The problem is that Kubernetes adds an extra layer. Remember that Containers run inside Pods. So while it is relatively easy and similar to what we have done before to set up a bind mount@comma{} this will only allow files to be shared between the insides of a container and the pod it is running on. The next necessary step is to somehow create a bridge from the pod's file system to the host's file system.

The first bit is similar to what we have done before: setup the container template to mount @code{/app/src}.

@example
containers:
- name: qfstandalone
  image: mickesv/qfstandalone:version1
  volumeMounts:
  - mountPath: /app/src
    name: source-dir
@end example

@cindex Kubernetes EmptyDir
This is coupled with a matching @code{volumes} statement for the pod. In the most basic@comma{} just create an empty directory inside the pod to match this volume:

@example
volumes:
- name: source-dir
  emptyDir: @{@}
@end example

@cindex Kubernetes HostPath
However@comma{} since the goal was to be able to reach the directory from the outside@comma{} we can go for the moonshot with a HostPath instead:

@example
volumes:
- name: source-dir
  hostPath:
    path: /app/src
    type: Directory
@end example

(The path can of course be anywhere here@comma{} it does not have to be @code{/app/src} since that only really makes sense inside the container. However@comma{} it is good to keep it short for the next step. In fact@comma{} it would not be a bad idea to keep it under @code{/tmp}.)

@cindex Kubernetes Node
Unfortunately@comma{} this @emph{still} does not mean that we are able to access the files. The reason for this is a Kubernetes concept which we have not discussed yet@comma{} namely that of a @emph{Node}. A Kubernetes node can be seen as the unit that would be run on one piece of (virtual) hardware. On this node pods@comma{} volumes@comma{} etc. are deployed. @code{minikube} is a single-node implementation of Kubernetes so there is not much more configuring to do@comma{} but if you deploy to a cloud provider you would get to decide how many nodes you want to run your application across@comma{} as well as how many volumes@comma{} pods@comma{} services@comma{} etc. that you desire.

You can access the inside of the single node with @code{minikube ssh} ; please take a moment to convince yourself that the minikube node is essentially a complete virtual machine.

The bottom line is that while we are able to access the inside of the running container with a hostPath volume@comma{} @emph{we are only able to do so from within the Kubernetes node}. Not quite what we were after@comma{} to put it mildly. It is actually worse than that@comma{} since when pods get deleted or moved over to another node@comma{} whatever was written to the hostPath directory gets left behind (hence why it would make sense to keep the mount point in @code{/tmp} ).

@example
+------------------------------------------+
| Node                                     |
|                                          |
|  +----------------+ +----------------+   |
|  | Pod            | | Pod            |   |
|  | +------------+ | |                |   |
|  | | Container  | | |                |   |
|  | +------------+ | |                |   |
|  | +------------+ | |                |   |
|  | | Container  | | |                |   |
|  | +------------+ | |                |   |
|  +----------------+ +----------------+   |
|                                          |
+------------------------------------------+
@end example

@cindex Network File System NFS
Despite all warnings about why this is a bad idea@comma{} if we still want to access the container's inside@comma{} there are two options available to us. One is to define a proper volume with a PersistentVolumeClaim for the container. Of course@comma{} there is a non-trivial amount of work to set this up in Kubernetes to ensure that an already existing directory on the host computer is made available as a PersistentVolume. A middle ground is perhaps to export the directory as an NFS share (or any other type of network filesystem if you are concerned with the security issues of NFS)@comma{} and mount this as the container volume.

The other option is to go the other direction and mount a local directory onto the Kubernetes node@comma{} and then use a hostPath setup to propagate this mount into the container. @code{minikube mount} is able to do this@comma{} but due to bugs this is not working at the time of writing.

@strong{Summary}
Accessing the running code of a container in Kubernetes is difficult and there are plenty of reasons to advice against it. The recommendation is to do the development using e.g. Docker Compose and only deploy to Kubernetes as a last step when everything is tested and ship-shape. This includes subsequent debugging: There should be nothing in the containers that wasn't there already when the image was created@comma{} and so all debugging can and should be done locally outside of the Kubernetes deployment.

@node Additional Concepts
@chapter Additional Concepts

@cindex Kubernetes SessionAffinity
A few loose ends need to be tied up to complete this introduction to Kubernetes. First@comma{} we are finally able to solve the previous problems that we had with scaling the frontend. If you remember@comma{} the use of @code{socket.io} meant that it was difficult to use a simple load-balancer to scale the frontend since each client-server connection relied on a socket to be kept alive for the duration. Well@comma{} one of the flags that can be given to a load-balancer service is @code{spec.sessionAffinity: ClientIP} @comma{} which means that Kubernetes will stick all sessions originating from the same client IP to the same deployment instance. This is all that is required in order to be able to scale the number of replicas of the qfstandalone deployment.

@cindex Kubernetes Scaling
One simple way to scale is to edit the @code{spec.replicas} in the yaml file and simply re-apply it. Go ahead; start the dashboard @code{minikube dashboard &} @comma{} edit the yaml file (add the @code{sessionAffinity} flag above while you're at it)@comma{} and re-run @code{kubectl apply -f qfstandalone-v-1-3.yaml} . You should see the number of deployed pods change as a consequence.

You can of course also scale directly via the command line: @code{kubectl scale --replicas=3 deployment/qfstandalone} .

@cindex Kubernetes Rollout
@cindex Kubernetes Liveness Probes
Keeping an eye on a running deployment is done with the dashboard -- you can often find clues in here about what is currently going on -- especially if some deployment is not rolling out as it should. If you want to programmatically keep track of the rollout status@comma{} there is the @code{kubectl rollout status} command. Especially when you have configured Readiness Probes (as we did before) or Liveness Probes@comma{} this is a useful way to keep tabs on how the scaling operation is going. Readiness Probes should be defined to ensure that new pods are well and truly booted and ready to start working @emph{before} Kubernetes adds them to the set of running deployments. If no Readiness Probes are defined@comma{} the pod may be expected to start serving requests before it is actually finished booting.

Liveness Probes are another useful addition@comma{} since they allow Kubernetes to automatically keep track of the health of the running deployment@comma{} and you may define rules about what should happen when a pod continues to report that it is not alive (or@comma{} to be precise@comma{} continuously fails to report that it is alive).

The @code{kubectl rollout} command can also be used to manage the deployment in some more detail. Most notably@comma{} it can be used to @code{pause} and @code{resume} parts of the deployment.

@cindex MongoDB Authentication
When we set up the MongoDB ReplicaSet we dodged the bullet of authentication. Technically@comma{} there are a number of steps involved in this that you really ought to try your hand at just because. First@comma{} the internal cluster should be told to use a specific key to communicate inside the cluster. Second@comma{} once the replicaset is up and running (or before? This used to work in one way and in a recent update it no longer works the same way)@comma{} you should setup users; One admin user to manage the databases and the database cluster@comma{} and at least one regular user that clients can use e.g. when querying the database.

@cindex Kubernetes Secrets
The shared key should of course not be bandied around like so much trash: If this key gets out then your database can become compromised by someone adding their own MongoDB instance to your ReplicaSet. As soon as the key is created@comma{} we should thus create a Kubernetes @emph{Secret} out of it@comma{} and then access this secret from within the containers. This is similar to how you can use data from ConfigMaps in your containers.

To summarise:

@example
# Generate and store the key as a Kubernetes Secret
openssl rand -base64 200 > ./replica-set-key.txt
kubectl create secret generic mongodb-bootstrap-data\
        --from-file=mongodb-keyfile=./replica-set-key.txt

# Add some users to the database
kubectl exec -i textstore-0\
        -- mongosh --quiet --eval 'db.getSiblingDB("admin")\
        .createUser(@{ user: "main_admin"@comma{} pwd: "hunter2"@comma{}\
                      roles: [ @{ role: "root"@comma{} db: "admin" @} ]@});'
kubectl exec -i textstore-0\
        -- mongosh --quiet --eval 'db.getSiblingDB("admin")\
        .auth("main_admin"@comma{} "hunter2"); \
        db.getSiblingDB("admin")\
        .createUser(@{ user: "kube"@comma{} pwd: "kubeuser"@comma{}\
                      roles: ["readWriteAnyDatabase"]@});'
@end example


@dots{} and the configuration of the MongoDB StatefulSet:

@example
  ---
  apiVersion: apps/v1
  kind: StatefulSet
  metadata:
    name: textstore
    labels:
      app: textstore
  spec:
    serviceName: textstore-service  # This becomes part of the hostname
        # (e.g. textstore-0.textstore-service.default.svc.cluster.local)
    replicas: 3
    selector:
      matchLabels:
        app: textstore
    template:
      metadata:
        labels:
          app: textstore
          startup: textstore-wait
      spec:
        containers:
        - name: textstore
          image: mongo
          command: ["mongod"@comma{} "--bind_ip"@comma{} "0.0.0.0"@comma{}
"--replSet"@comma{} "MainRepSet"@comma{} "--auth"@comma{} "--clusterAuthMode"@comma{}
"keyFile"@comma{} "--keyFile"@comma{} "/etc/secrets-volume/mongodb-keyfile"@comma{}
"--setParameter"@comma{} "authenticationMechanisms=SCRAM-SHA-1"]
          readinessProbe:
            exec:
              command: ["mongosh"@comma{} "--eval"@comma{} "db.adminCommand(@{ping: 1@})"]
          ports:
          - containerPort: 27017
            name: mongodb-port
          volumeMounts:
          - name: textstore-data
            mountPath: /data/db
          - name: mongo-config
            mountPath: /data/configdb
          - name: secrets-volume
            readOnly: true
            mountPath: /etc/secrets-volume
        volumes:
        - name: secrets-volume
          secret:
            secretName: mongodb-bootstrap-data
            defaultMode: 256
    volumeClaimTemplates:
      - metadata:
          name: textstore-data
        spec:
          accessModes: ["ReadWriteOnce"]
          resources:
            requests: 
              storage: 10Mi
      - metadata:
          name: mongo-config
        spec:
          accessModes: ["ReadWriteOnce"]
          resources:
            requests: 
              storage: 5Mi          
  ---
@end example

@cindex Lambda Functions
@cindex Azure Functions
@cindex Azure Durable Functions
One final trend to keep an eye on is that of @emph{lambda functions}. They are not always called that@comma{} e.g. Microsoft refers to them as Azure Functions or Azure Durable Functions@comma{} but the gist is the same. In some ways@comma{} this is a natural extension of the microservice architecture@comma{} when even a REST API can be considered too much@comma{} and all that is needed is a way to replicate and run a function in a distributed way. Typically@comma{} the desire is to develop locally and then seamlessly decide whether to run functions within in the same process@comma{} perhaps run them on a high-performance computing unit in the same host computer (for example a graphics card)@comma{} or to scale out and run an arbitrary number of replicas of the function at the cloud provider. This pattern is not always applicable@comma{} but for @emph{some} computing tasks it really lends itself to creating a transparent and yet scalable solution.

@node Summary
@chapter Summary

This is it! We have reached the end of the road for what I wanted to cover in this part of the course. (See @ref{Learning Outcomes} for a list of the relevant Learning Outcomes)  To begin with@comma{} the following concepts have been explored:

@itemize
@item
Microservices as a Software Architecture Design Decision
@item
Principles of Microservice Architectures
@item
Cloud Application Architecture Patterns
@item
Microservices and REST APIs
@end itemize

A software architecture consists of the design decisions that underpin it. Once the decision has been taken to build the application as a collection of microservices (and the rationale for this has been captured and documented)@comma{} there are a number of design decisions that follow@comma{} including how separate components in the architecture should collaborate to solve the overall task@comma{} and some design principles and business decisions are given as a consequence. To some extent@comma{} much of the remaining work is simply to instantiate these already given design decisions in the best possible way@comma{} to leverage as much business use as possible out of them.

From these overall design decisions we dive deeper into more specific technologies@comma{} i.e.:

@itemize
@item
Hypervisors and Lightweight Virtual Machines
@item
Provisioning using shell scripting
@item
Provisioning using dedicated provisioning tools
@item
Orchestration and Deployment of a complete microservice solution
@end itemize

Specifically@comma{} we use some specific tools:

@itemize
@item
Docker@comma{} as a means to define a single microservice
@item
Docker Compose@comma{} to orchestrate a complete local solution
@item
Kubernetes@comma{} to orchestrate and deploy a solution possibly to a cloud provider
@end itemize

These technologies and tools are typical representatives of contemporary software microservice development. They are by no means alone on the market@comma{} there are plenty of other competing technologies@comma{} but with this stack and an understanding of the concepts behind the tools you are well equipped to face the future and any tool @emph{du jour} life may throw at you.

While exploring these tools@comma{} there is one key principle that have permeated every step along the way: @emph{Infrastructure as Code} . Only by treating the infrastructure@comma{} the deployment@comma{} in the same way as the rest of an applications code-base@comma{} are we able to maintain repeatable orchestrations@comma{} where each individual part of a deployment is configuration managed and tested.

In fact@comma{} this is important so let me repeat myself:

@emph{Infrastructure is Code}

Here's a bunny to help you remember:

@example

 ________________________
( Infrastructure is Code )
 ------------------------
      o
(\/) o
(..)
c(")(")

@end example

The ``only'' thing remaining for you to do now is to prove that you too have learnt all this. Read on to figure out how.

@node Assignment Build Something
@chapter Assignment: Build Something

@anchor{assignment-build-something}

It is time to put everything you have learnt so far into practice. The goal of this assignment is for you to create a microservice-based application according to the following principles:

@enumerate
@item
The application must be deployable using Kubernetes.
@item
The application must consist of at least two different types of microservices and a database.
@item
Each microservice must implement a REST API for access
@item
The application must be accessible from ``the outside'' of kubernetes (e.g. via a web browser)
@item
All microservices in the application must be horizontally scalable independently from each other.
@item
Any microservice images required to run your application must have been pushed to docker hub so that Kubernetes may find them.
@item
The application must make use of some form of database running as a separate microservice.
@item
The database must use storage which is persistent across restarts of the deployment infrastructure.
@item
The database does not have to be scalable.
@end enumerate

The following items shall be delivered:

@enumerate
@item
A description of the software you have built and what it does.
@item
A software architecture design of your application@comma{} describing the role of each component in your system@comma{} their responsibilities@comma{} and the architecture principles (e.g. cloud architecture patterns) that are used to connect them to a functioning system. This also includes a mapping between software components and the microservices designed and built to implement the components.
@item
A discussion of the benefits and challenges with your architecture design. This must include a discussion about security. It must also include a discussion of what you have done or what can be done to mitigate the identified challenges.
@item
A link to a configuration management repository (e.g. git) where the source code of the application can be viewed. This must also include the code for your Kubernetes deployment.
@end enumerate

Once delivered@comma{} @emph{a meeting will be scheduled} where you are expected to run and explain your application and its deployment. Be prepared to answer questions about:

@itemize
@item
Your software application idea
@item
The architecture design decisions in your application
@item
The business implications of these architecture decisions
@item
Interaction between different microservices
@item
Details of your deployment
@item
Security issues identified and/or mitigated
@end itemize

If you prefer@comma{} you may also submit a video recording (5-10 min long) where you go through what the project is abut@comma{} what the separate microservices do@comma{} run the project and access it through its user interface (e.g. a web browser)@comma{} show any log output@comma{} and briefly walk through your kubernetes yaml files.

Your application:
@itemize
@item
must display your ability to progamatically connect to and use a REST API@.
@item
must display your ability to program a microservice that provides a REST API@.
@item
must not reuse existing microservices that have not been developed by yourself (Except the database container).
@item
may implement any software idea; it may also re-use the @emph{idea} from an already existing software.
@item
may be too small or trivial to actually warrant scaling; please acknowledge this and provide instructions for what needs to be pretended for the application to make sense.
@item
may use any programming language.
@item
may use a different access interface than a web browser@comma{} as long as no additional tools need to be installed on my computer.
@item
may use any type of database (e.g. NoSQL@comma{} SQL@comma{} Graph@comma{} etc.).
@item
may be deployed to a cloud provider already.
@end itemize

There is a Quiz on Canvas where you can submit your article summaries and answers to the questions.

@strong{NOTICE:} This is a @emph{marked} assignment that contributes to the grade on the course. Specifically@comma{} the assignment contribute to your grade on the following parts of the course:
@itemize
@item
Cloud Provisioning and Deployment
@item
The Business Case for Cloud Computing
@end itemize

